 none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimectlflood</span><span class="ttl">Guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() on CRIME_CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits -- and latching it REDS A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW</span><span class="tip">Guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() on CRIME_CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits -- and latching it REDS A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW — note: filed 2026-09-17; NOT a free tidy-up -- the fix owes the oracle-correction ORDER</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — dev_sgi_ip32.c:377-379: `if (idata != 0) fatal(&quot;[ CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented control 0x%016llx ]&quot;, ...)&#x27; -- every CONTROL write carrying a bit the model does not implement prints, with no latch and no `(once)&#x27; marker.  Same class as pit8253flood; belongs to floodclass. *** THE REASON TO FILE IT RATHER THAN FIX IT: LATCHING IT WOULD RED A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW, AND THE COUPLING IS EXACT. ***  regress/crime_width_probe.py:1256 is `row(&quot;p acc ctl msg&quot;, count(t, &quot;CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented&quot;, clean), 3, ...)&#x27; -- it COUNTS this very message and expects THREE, one per accepted CONTROL write (0x01 sd, 0x0001 sw, 0x0201 sw).  A latch takes that count to ONE and reds the row. SO WHOEVER TAKES IT MUST USE THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER: re-pin the row FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new expectation, THEN change the code.  Otherwise the fix and its detector land together and nothing distinguishes a repair from a test fitted to a binary. LOW-MED because the flood is bounded in practice: the message fires only on CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits, which the #529/#530 probes drive deliberately and a real guest driver largely does not. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md tail entry; regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/opus_pass1.md (the seat that surfaced it)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimeramexit</span><span class="ttl">Two surviving exit(1) in DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)&#x27;s RAM-size prelude -- machine-config gated, not guest-drivable</span><span class="tip">Two surviving exit(1) in DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)&#x27;s RAM-size prelude -- machine-config gated, not guest-drivable — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable&#x27;s ruling: not this round&#x27;s fix, but not a silence either</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-ON-RULING</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Found by the Opus measuring seat during repair 14 and FILED ON FABLE&#x27;S EXPLICIT DIRECTION: *`not this round&#x27; is a FILE, not a SILENCE* -- the seat found them and the findings record carried no disposition. TWO exit(1) survive inside the very function #529 de-fatalised: `physical_ram_in_mb &gt; 1024&#x27; and `physical_ram_in_mb % mb_per_bank&#x27;. *** A CITATION NOTE WORTH KEEPING, because two seats gave different line numbers and BOTH WERE RIGHT: Fable cited :244/:255 and Opus cited :246/:259.  The GUARDS are at 244 and 255; the exit(1) CALLS are at 246 and 259.  They were citing different things, not disagreeing. *** NOT GUEST-DRIVABLE and that is why it is filed rather than fixed: `cpu-&gt;machine-&gt;physical_ram_in_mb&#x27; is set at machine CONSTRUCTION from the -M option, and no guest access reaches it.  So this is a host-usability defect (a bad -M ends the process instead of being rejected with a message), not a guest-reachable one.  The contract should SAY SO -- Fable&#x27;s point is that an undisposed finding in a de-fatalisation round reads as an oversight. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c guards at :244 and :255, exit(1) at :246 and :259</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimesplitwidth</span><span class="ttl">Split widths 3/5/6/7 unreachable at CRIME only because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is a multiple of 8 -- no rows if that changes</span><span class="tip">Split widths 3/5/6/7 unreachable at CRIME only because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is a multiple of 8 -- no rows if that changes — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable ruling item (6); LATENT, stated as a CONDITION not a count</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-ON-RULING</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILED ON FABLE ORDER ITEM (6).  A real MIPS guest emits len in {1,2,4,8} exactly, BUT memory_rw.c:523 CLAMPS a straddling access at a device end -- len = length - paddr -- which can be 1..7 in general.  It is inert at CRIME ONLY because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is 0x280 = 8 x 80, so for a naturally aligned access the clamp is always a multiple of the width.  **Change that one define to a non-multiple of 8 and widths 3/5/6/7 become reachable at a handler whose detector has never seen them.**  The FIX is almost certainly correct for them -- the decline arm treats every non-4, non-8 width alike -- what is missing is any row that would notice if it were not. *** THE REASSURING HALF, recorded so nobody re-derives it: the constant is NOT free to change, because :77 sizes the register array with it (reg[DEV_CRIME_LENGTH / sizeof(uint64_t)]), so a non-multiple of 8 would also truncate the array.  A future edit must defeat both. *** PROVENANCE is a three-cornered disagreement that ended in a CONDITION rather than a number: deepseek wanted the lane and PC as free factors; codex read the producer and showed the domain is REACHABILITY-CONSTRAINED; the Opus adjudication then showed codex own argument is CONDITIONAL on this constant.  Fable keeps D1 OPEN and says explicitly to state it as a condition, not as 2. LOW and LATENT: no witness, no reachable path today, and rowing it now would add rows for a state the machine cannot enter -- the cannot-hurt the addition licence refuses. — evidence: dev_sgi_ip32.c:75 DEV_CRIME_LENGTH 0x280 and :77 the array sized by it; memory_rw.c:523 the clamp</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pciaddrreadclobber</span><span class="ttl">A READ of PCI ADDR (0xcf8) decomposes idata=0 and clobbers the latched address -- in ALL THREE PCI bridges, so an upstream idiom rather than a macepci bug</span><span class="tip">A READ of PCI ADDR (0xcf8) decomposes idata=0 and clobbers the latched address -- in ALL THREE PCI bridges, so an upstream idiom rather than a macepci bug — note: filed 2026-09-17 while triaging macepciwidth severity; LATENT, NO WITNESS and none attempted; a CLASS across three files, fix all or none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — A READ of the PCI ADDR register runs the WRITE path.  idata is declared = 0 and assigned ONLY inside if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE), and the 0xcf8 arm tests writeflag NOWHERE -- so a read decomposes ZERO, latches bus 0 / device 0 / function 0 / register 0, and returns 0.  It both answers wrongly and CLOBBERS the address a previous write latched.  *** THE SIBLING CENSUS IS THE FINDING AND IT REVERSES THE OBVIOUS READING. ***  This looked like a macepci outlier; it is not.  ALL THREE PCI bridges in the tree share the shape -- dev_sgi_ip32.c (macepci, case 0xcf8), dev_cpc700.c (cpc700_pci, case 0) and dev_eagle.c (eagle, case 0) -- so it is an UPSTREAM IDIOM rather than damage this fork did, and a round fixing macepci alone would leave the class open in two files while making the tree inconsistent.  The fix lands in all three or in none.  LOW because the damage needs a guest that READS 0xcf8 between writing it and reading 0xcfc; the common driver idiom is write-ADDR then read-DATA, which never touches this path.  NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE WAS ATTEMPTED -- recorded as not-yet-attempted rather than as absence, because those are different states.  Found while triaging macepciwidth&#x27;s SEVERITY, not while looking for it.  *** AND THE TRIAGE ITSELF CAME BACK NEGATIVE, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THIS ENTRY: macepciwidth STAYS MED. ***  Its len != 4 fall-through is NOT memory-unsafe, now CHECKED rather than assumed -- memory_readmax64 and memory_writemax64 are both bounded by len, the only array index reachable through bus_pci_data_access is masked (cfg_base[ofs &amp; (PCI_CFG_MEM_SIZE - 1)]), and config writes go through the dev-&gt;cfg_reg_write callback rather than a direct index.  The cost is an unlatched flood plus wrong-value semantics, which is correctness and not memory safety.  The macepciwidth entry&#x27;s phrase &#x27;the sibling is WORSE&#x27; is true of the DECLINE BEHAVIOUR and FALSE of severity; recorded so a future round does not read it as grounds to escalate. — evidence: src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) case 0xcf8; src/devices/dev_cpc700.c case 0; src/devices/dev_eagle.c case 0; src/devices/bus_pci.c bus_pci_data_access</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253lsbnotimer</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c one-byte formats: BOTH arms keep the other byte stale against the datasheet, and the LSB arm additionally installs no timer -- wrong host rate on counter 0</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c one-byte formats: BOTH arms keep the other byte stale against the datasheet, and the LSB arm additionally installs no timer -- wrong host rate on counter 0 — note: filed 2026-09-17 by the kimi-k3 seat, WIDENED the same day by Fable 5.1&#x27;s adjudication from a datasheet passage I read past</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THIS ROW WAS UNDER-SCOPED AND THE DATASHEET QUESTION IT FILED AS OPEN IS ANSWERED LOCALLY. ***  Fable found the governing sentence at _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172: &#x27;CRM and CRL are cleared when the Counter is programmed.  In this way if the Counter has been programmed for one byte counts (either most significant byte only or least significant byte only) the other byte will be zero.&#x27;  THE MODEL DOES THE OPPOSITE IN BOTH ONE-BYTE ARMS -- :175 masks with 0xff00 and keeps a STALE MSB, :179 masks with 0x00ff and keeps a STALE LSB -- where the datasheet requires the other byte to be ZERO.  So the defect is not only &#x27;the LSB arm starts no timer&#x27;: THE COMPOSED COUNT IS WRONG IN BOTH ARMS, and the MSB-only arm is the one that DOES compute hz and call timer_add/timer_update_frequency, so a stale low byte yields a WRONG HOST TIMER RATE ON COUNTER 0 -- the same consequence as pit8253bcdbinary, reached by a different route.  Severity raised MED -&gt; MED-HIGH accordingly: the original filing described the weaker half.  The datasheet also names the FIX SHAPE rather than leaving it to design: clear counter[sel] at the CONTROL WORD (dev_8253.c:310-312), which is exactly where the datasheet says the CR is cleared.  *** I HAD READ THIS DATASHEET AN HOUR EARLIER, FOR THIS ROW, AND WALKED PAST THE PASSAGE. ***  I grepped for &#x27;BCD&#x27; and read the hits; the CR-clearing rule is 500 lines away and mentions neither BCD nor LSB in terms my grep matched.  A targeted grep over a primary source answers the question you asked it, not the question you should have asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172; src/devices/dev_8253.c:175, :179, :181-184, :192-196 — record: 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this row.  Same provenance as `pit8253bcdbinary`: the entries cite `panel_20260917_063557`, convened on `pit8253flood`.  Three recorded seats here, which is again why a count cannot do this sorting.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PRIMARY SOURCE MAKES THIS WORSE THAN FILED.  _scratchpad/i8254.txt :219 lists RW1 RW0 = 0 1 as &#x27;Read Write least significant byte only&#x27;, a first-class mode beside MSB-only and 16-bit.  And :528 states the conventions for ALL mode timing diagrams: &#x27;Counters are programmed for binary (not BCD) counting and for reading writing least significant byte (LSB) only&#x27;.  *** EVERY MODE TIMING DIAGRAM IN THE INTEL DATASHEET IS DRAWN IN LSB-ONLY MODE. ***  It is the manufacturer&#x27;s canonical illustration case, not an exotic corner -- and it is the one programming mode in which this model never starts a timer. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt (Intel 8254 datasheet); _scratchpad/i8254.pdf present since 2026-08-15</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — A counter programmed in LSB-ONLY mode (RW=01) is SILENTLY DEAD: the LSB arm updates counter[] and nothing else -- no hz[] recompute, no timer_add, no timer_update_frequency, and no diagnostic -- while the MSB arm does all three.  RW=01 is a LEGITIMATE 8253 mode (I8253_TIMER_LSB 0x10), so this is not a refusal decision: the model implements one half of a two-half register and not the other.  It is option-set member 7 (IMPLEMENT), not a latch question.  TWO consequences and the second is arguably worse: a counter programmed LSB-only never starts, silently, and for counter 0 that is the interrupt source; and if a timer was ALREADY installed by an earlier MSB write, a later LSB-only write changes counter[] while the running timer keeps the OLD frequency, so recorded counter and actual rate DIVERGE with no diagnostic.  *** FOUND BY A PACKET-FED SEAT (kimi-k3:cloud) FROM THE INLINED FUNCTION ALONE, while answering a brief about the flood. ***  Verified by reading before filing; the seat&#x27;s claim was exact.  The design question -- should the LSB arm install the timer by symmetry, or does the real 8253 defer until both halves are written in 16-bit mode -- is a DATASHEET question, and _scratchpad/i8254.pdf and i8254.txt are present locally, so it is answerable without guessing.  Caution for the row author: an MSB write to counter 0 calls timer_add with hz = 1193182/counter, so a SMALL counter installs a HIGH-FREQUENCY host timer; keep it large. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ollama_kimi.txt; src/devices/dev_8253.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253bcdbinary</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c accepts a BCD control word then reads the count as BINARY -- a BCD counter runs at the wrong rate, and for counter 0 that is the host timer (~4x)</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c accepts a BCD control word then reads the count as BINARY -- a BCD counter runs at the wrong rate, and for counter 0 that is the host timer (~4x) — note: filed 2026-09-17 from pit8253flood pass 1; LIVE and guest-visible.  Severity corrected MED-HIGH -&gt; MED by Fable 5.1 on REACH: no real-guest driver found.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — SEVERITY CORRECTED MED-HIGH -&gt; MED, and the correction is about REACH rather than impact.  The impact stands exactly as filed -- a BCD counter decoded as binary puts counter 0&#x27;s host timer ~4x off -- but codex searched the guest sources and found NO guest that programs BCD, and none that programs counter 1 at all.  I had set MED-HIGH on impact alone without weighing whether anything reaches it, which is the same mistake as ranking by witness rung instead of by harm, inverted.  *** AND THE CONSEQUENCE I ATTACHED TO THIS ROW WAS OVERTURNED: see pit8253flood.  I had ruled &#x27;do not latch :342 because it is the only surviving symptom&#x27;; Fable ruled that confuses a BOUNDED ANNOUNCE with SILENCE, and that repetition carries no information once the defect is FILED -- which it now is, by this row. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/pit8253_adjudication.md; panel_20260917_063557/ — record: 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this row.  Its entries cite `panel_20260917_063557`, whose brief is `PASS 1 -- pit8253flood: seven unlatched guest-drivable fatal() sites`: a panel on `pit8253flood`, a DIFFERENT row.  Seats naming `pit8253bcdbinary` there were pointing at sibling defects in the same file, which is a finding worth keeping and not a stage.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PRIMARY SOURCE, read the same day rather than cited from a paragraph: _scratchpad/i8254.txt :213-216 gives the control word&#x27;s BCD bit as 0 = Binary Counter 16-bits and 1 = Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) Counter (4 Decades); :671-673 says the largest initial count 0 is equivalent to 2^16 binary and 10^4 BCD; :677-678 says the counter wraps to FFFF hex for binary or 9999 for BCD.  THE DATASHEET THEREFORE STATES THE DEFECT DIRECTLY -- a BCD counter is a 4-decade counter and the model applies 16-bit binary arithmetic to the same bytes.  The Opus seat&#x27;s 0x9999 example is the datasheet&#x27;s own maximum BCD count, not an arbitrary worst case.  A round taking this row does not need to reason about what BCD means. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt (Intel 8254 datasheet); _scratchpad/i8254.pdf present since 2026-08-15</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pitmodebyteshared</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c keeps ONE mode_byte for all three counters, so programming any counter overwrites the RW format of the other two</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c keeps ONE mode_byte for all three counters, so programming any counter overwrites the RW format of the other two — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable 5.1&#x27;s records finding: this defect had NO ledger row and existed only as a residual bullet under #440&#x27;s CHANGELOG block</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** A RECORDS DEFECT FOUND BY THE ADJUDICATOR, AND IT IS THE BURIED-BY-CLOSURE SHAPE THIS PROJECT ALREADY RECORDS. ***  Two different defects were travelling under the id &#x27;pitclobber&#x27;.  The LEDGER row of that name is #439&#x27;s -- an 8253 LATCH command clobbering the counter&#x27;s programmed RW format -- and it is state &#x27;in work&#x27; with a hold text reading FIX SHIPPED (#439) and a state_note &#x27;Recommend CLOSE&#x27;.  The SHARED-mode_byte-ACROSS-COUNTERS defect, which codex&#x27;s witness-ordering constraint depends on and which the panel packet called &#x27;pitclobber&#x27;, had NO LEDGER ROW AT ALL -- it survived only as a residual bullet at OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:6693 under #440&#x27;s block.  **Closing the pitclobber row would therefore have buried a live defect that no row carried.**  THE DEFECT ITSELF: mode_byte is ONE field (dev_8253.c:78) written at exactly one site (:310), so a control word programming ANY counter replaces the RW format of all three.  That is why :204/:270 fire only before the first non-latch control word, and it is why a device-signature control word on another counter is NOT neutral for a witness -- the constraint codex raised.  It needs its own id before any round is built on it, which is what this row is. — evidence: src/devices/dev_8253.c:78 (the field), :310 (its only writer); OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:6693</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmaxferflood</span><span class="ttl">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c&#x27;s &#x27;Transfering %i bytes&#x27; fatal() is UNLATCHED on the NORMAL DMA path, so it floods on every legal guest transfer</span><span class="tip">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c&#x27;s &#x27;Transfering %i bytes&#x27; fatal() is UNLATCHED on the NORMAL DMA path, so it floods on every legal guest transfer — note: filed 2026-09-17 by the agy seat during the late-review panel on the six unreviewed fixes -- found while auditing a DIFFERENT question, the standing license working as intended.  *** DISPOSITION IS LATE-DISSENT 3 (file as a new item), NOT an L2 against #462, and the reason is MEASURED not procedural: `git show 7c9dd9e^:src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c | grep -n Transfering` returns the identical unlatched fatal() at :825.  #462 MOVED and re-pointed it (at d-&gt;data_len rather than `length`); it did not introduce it. ***  NOT the one-line change it looks like: the flood is on the SUCCESS path, so the (once) idiom is the wrong shape -- latching would suppress genuinely per-transfer information, and the row `nodata notx` PINS this line MEASURED RED at 50/51 (probe_pre51.raw), so a latch MOVES that row.  The option set needs naming before a fix is designed.  The file already carries 12 correctly latched sites and an enumerated GDROM_W_* bit-position scheme built for this.  *** UPHELD MED by the Fable adjudication 2026-09-17 (9 of 9), which verified the parent at `7c9dd9e^:825&#x27; shows the same unlatched fatal() above the NULL test -- #462 MOVED it and CHANGED ITS ARGUMENTS.  ***  AND A COUPLING THAT MUST BE WRITTEN INTO ANY FIX FOR THIS ROW: the Opus seat&#x27;s finding (b) proposes making `accept dma&#x27; PARSE THE NUMBER on this very line (`Transfering (\d+) bytes&#x27; asserting 408), because no row reads it today.  **So whoever later LATCHES or DEMOTES this line must re-pin that row IN ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER** -- re-pin first, measure the committed build RED under the new expectation, then change the code.  Fable calls this &#x27;the crimectlflood coupling again&#x27;.  Two rows, one line, and they move in opposite directions: one wants the line QUIETER, the other wants it PARSED.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — agy&#x27;s words: the line &#x27;is UNLATCHED and executes on EVERY legal guest DMA transfer.  It is guest-drivable and constitutes a log flood under normal driver operation.&#x27;  VERIFIED, and the provenance check CHANGED THE DISPOSITION: the pre-#462 file carries the same unlatched call, so this is a pre-existing condition surfaced by the review, not a regression the review caught.  Filed rather than fixed in-round per the late-dissent rule -- closure changes the LICENSE, never the truth-value, and this is &#x27;everything else&#x27;. — evidence: src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1497 (the unlatched fatal, post-move); 7c9dd9e^:825 (the same line, already unlatched, pre-move); src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:224-243 (the GDROM_W_* scheme it does not use); _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">cpusexitsweep</span><span class="ttl">exitsweep censused terminating sites across src/devices/ ONLY -- src/cpus/ was never covered, and cpu-&gt;running = 0 is a second shape an exit(-keyed sweep cannot see</span><span class="tip">exitsweep censused terminating sites across src/devices/ ONLY -- src/cpus/ was never covered, and cpu-&gt;running = 0 is a second shape an exit(-keyed sweep cannot see — note: filed 2026-09-17.  The gap is in the CLOSED exitsweep row&#x27;s own title: &#x27;terminating sites across src/devices/&#x27;.  src/cpus/ holds 111 exit( across 23 files, 1 abort(, and 52 `cpu-&gt;running = 0&#x27; across 10 files (deriver: grep -roh over src/cpus/*.c; RE-RUN, do not quote). *** THE COUNT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND NOT THE CLASS -- PAL_halt is a legitimate halt; what defines the class is reachability from a GUEST-SUPPLIED SELECTOR, which no regex tests. ***  Verified instances: alpha_prom_call() reads a0-a3 from guest registers and BOTH its default: arms do fatal() + cpu-&gt;running = 0, so a guest setting a register and making a PAL call stops the emulator.  Ranked HIGH for the CENSUS, not for any single site.  Owes a src/cpus/ census on the same footing exitsweep gave src/devices/, keyed on guest reachability, each site triaged under the seven-member option set.  *** FIGURES CORRECTED WITHIN THE HOUR: the filed 111 exit( / 52 running=0 were the RAW STRING COUNT and included this project&#x27;s OWN COMMENTS about the pattern.  Stripping block comments, line comments and string literals gives **73 exit( and 41 running=0 IN CODE**.  FOUR files (cpu_mips_instr.c 5, cpu_sh_instr.c 2, cpu_ppc_instr.c 1, cpu_m88k_instr.c 1) have ZERO real sites and were listed as having them.  Caught because cpu_alpha_palcode.c:413 enumerated as a site and is the COMMENT that documents the other sites -- the census counted its own documentation.  THE CLASS IS SHARPER THAN &#x27;keyed on a correlate&#x27;: a string-keyed census is inflated by its own prose whenever the codebase comments on the pattern being counted, and a de-fatalisation fork is the worst case for that.  The SUBSTANCE is unchanged -- src/cpus/ is still uncensused and both palcode arms are still guest-drivable halts. ***  *** RELABELLED RESEARCH (was HIGH) by the Fable adjudication 2026-09-17: &#x27;HIGH is a defect severity; a census has no witness and is a research item that generates rows.  Label it RESEARCH with a priority, not a severity.&#x27;  The deriver being stated is right and is kept.  STILL OWED: the FOUR-&gt;FIVE L4 at cpu_alpha_palcode.c -- the in-file comment still says FOUR sites and the triage found FIVE. ***  *** `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s CENSUS IS SPELLING-KEYED AND CANNOT SEE A THIRD OF ITS OWN POPULATION. The concealment is THREE-LAYERED, and I verified all three myself. ***  **(1) `running = 0` vs `running = false`.**  Measured in `src/cpus/`: **`= 0` gives 54 hits across 11 files; `= false` gives 20 hits across 10 files.**  A `= 0`-keyed census misses every one of the 20 -- and the files it misses include `cpu_dyntrans.c`, `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c`, `cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c` and `cpu_sh_instr.c`, which is **four files the sweeping seat&#x27;s own table did not list either**.  The `= false` population is the m88k / i960 / RISC-V / MIPS-reboot family. **(2) `ABORT_EXECUTION` IS A MACRO, so neither spelling appears at the USE sites.**  It is defined three times -- `cpu_i960_instr.c:45`, `cpu_m88k_instr.c:45`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:45` -- and every invocation is a bare `ABORT_EXECUTION`.  A grep for either assignment spelling finds the three definitions and none of the uses. **(3) COMMENTS AND `#if 0` INFLATE BOTH SPELLINGS**, so the raw hit counts above are an upper bound, not the live population -- the seat stripped them programmatically and found, for example, that `cpu_dyntrans.c` has ONE live `= 0` (the `bad` label) with the other two commented out, and that `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c`&#x27;s alignment halt sits in the `#else` of an `#if 1`. **THIS IS THE SESSION&#x27;S DOMINANT FAILURE FAMILY ARRIVING IN A SHIPPED CENSUS ROW:** the deriver measured a CORRELATE -- one way of spelling the assignment -- rather than the thing, which is *does this code path stop the emulated CPU*.  The carrier already states the rule (*&#x27;before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member&#x27;*) and `staticlatchscope` is the recorded precedent where three name-keyed censuses of one class all came back wrong. **THE FIX IS A RE-DERIVATION, NOT A SWEEP.**  `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s number is owed a re-run keyed on the three spellings together, with comments and `#if 0` stripped and macro uses expanded. No code changes; the row&#x27;s count is the deliverable.  *** `abort()` IS ABSENT FROM `src/cpus/`: ZERO CALLS.  An empty result with its denominator, and it CLOSES that class for this domain. ***  The only hit is a COMMENT at `memory_alpha.c:195` -- *&#x27;must not abort()/exit() the host&#x27;* -- which documents a host death `#194` REMOVED, not a live one.  `cpu_mips_instr.c` has a second comment, prose about `cpu-&gt;running = 0`.  Lookalikes that a careless grep counts: `abortdyntrans` (an ARM dyntrans helper), `abort_dump:` (an m88k `rte` label), and &#x27;abort execution&#x27; in prose. *** AND I REPORTED &#x27;1&#x27; FOR THIS EARLIER TODAY BY GREPPING `abort(` AND COUNTING A LINE OF PROSE.  Same correlate-keying failure, mine, in the same session that keeps finding it. *** Denominator: every `.c`/`.h` under `src/cpus/`, including the `#include`d `.c` files and the generated `tmp_*`.  Also checked and absent: `raise(`, `SIGABRT`, `__builtin_trap` (the `raise` hits are `ppc_fpscr_raise` and `*_interrupt_assert`). Related but NOT this class: `cpu_dyntrans.c`&#x27;s two `assert()`s on page-aligned translation inserts, which can only abort with `NDEBUG` off -- and this tree builds with it on -- and which pin a HOST invariant rather than a guest opcode. **NO MAPLE-STYLE UNBOUNDED INSTRUCTION-PATH LOOP EXISTS in `src/cpus/`** either.  The one shared &#x27;I do not know this instruction&#x27; halt is `cpu_dyntrans.c`&#x27;s `bad` label, which is included into every dyntrans CPU -- so it is one site with many faces rather than many sites.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this stage.  Two entries (grok, opus5) recorded 2026-09-18 from the unread-seat sweep, neither citing a panel dir.  `cpusexitsweep` is a census row that seats raise while reviewing other `exit(` work; the remarks are about it, not a panel on it.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** TRIAGE OF THE MOTIVATING FILE: FIVE LIVE SITES, ALL FIVE GUEST-DRIVABLE. ***  :138 (a1, GETENV id), :149 (a0, PROM call number), :189 (palcode called in userMode), :443 (a1, wrent kentry selector), :500 (palcode, top-level dispatch default).  a0-a3 come straight from cpu-&gt;cd.alpha.r[] and palcode is an instruction field, so each is reachable by a guest setting a register or executing call_pal.  *** THE CENSUS NEEDED A FOURTH REFINEMENT: `#if 0&#x27; REGIONS ARE NOT COMPILED. ***  :474 (PAL_bugchk), :478 (PAL_OSF1_syscall) and :487 (KENTRY not set) sit inside #if 0...#endif -- controlled by reading the boundaries at :472/:480.  Tree-wide the progression is 53 raw -&gt; 41 code -&gt; **38 LIVE** for running=0, and 111 -&gt; 73 -&gt; **73** for exit( (no #if 0 effect there).  ALL THREE #if 0 LOSSES ARE IN THIS FILE.  *** AND THE FILE&#x27;S OWN COMMENT NAMES FOUR, NOT FIVE -- it omits :500, the top-level PALcode default, which meets an unrecognised selector in exactly the sense its sentence describes.  Whether that was a deliberate scoping of &#x27;selector&#x27; to the PAL-call ARGUMENTS or an omission is NOT established, and this claims only what was counted -- but a reader trusting &#x27;four&#x27; misses a live guest-drivable halt, and that comment was the only record of the class until today. ***  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: that any of the five is a BUG (halting on an unimplemented PAL code may be right; DELETE is one of seven options), that the other 33 live sites are reachable (untriaged; cpu_arm.c alone has 16), or any severity ranking. — evidence: regress/artifacts/cpusexitsweep/TRIAGE_palcode_20260917.md; src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c :138 :149 :189 :443 :500 (live) and :472-480, :484-495 (the #if 0 regions holding :474 :478 :487)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s CENSUS IS SPELLING-KEYED AND CANNOT SEE A THIRD OF ITS OWN POPULATION. The concealment is THREE-LAYERED, and I verified all three myself. ***  **(1) `running = 0` vs `running = false`.**  Measured in `src/cpus/`: **`= 0` gives 54 hits across 11 files; `= false` gives 20 hits across 10 files.**  A `= 0`-keyed census misses every one of the 20 -- and the files it misses include `cpu_dyntrans.c`, `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c`, `cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c` and `cpu_sh_instr.c`, which is **four files the sweeping seat&#x27;s own table did not list either**.  The `= false` population is the m88k / i960 / RISC-V / MIPS-reboot family. **(2) `ABORT_EXECUTION` IS A MACRO, so neither spelling appears at the USE sites.**  It is defined three times -- `cpu_i960_instr.c:45`, `cpu_m88k_instr.c:45`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:45` -- and every invocation is a bare `ABORT_EXECUTION`.  A grep for either assignment spelling finds the three definitions and none of the uses. **(3) COMMENTS AND `#if 0` INFLATE BOTH SPELLINGS**, so the raw hit counts above are an upper bound, not the live population -- the seat stripped them programmatically and found, for example, that `cpu_dyntrans.c` has ONE live `= 0` (the `bad` label) with the other two commented out, and that `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c`&#x27;s alignment halt sits in the `#else` of an `#if 1`. **THIS IS THE SESSION&#x27;S DOMINANT FAILURE FAMILY ARRIVING IN A SHIPPED CENSUS ROW:** the deriver measured a CORRELATE -- one way of spelling the assignment -- rather than the thing, which is *does this code path stop the emulated CPU*.  The carrier already states the rule (*&#x27;before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member&#x27;*) and `staticlatchscope` is the recorded precedent where three name-keyed censuses of one class all came back wrong. **THE FIX IS A RE-DERIVATION, NOT A SWEEP.**  `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s number is owed a re-run keyed on the three spellings together, with comments and `#if 0` stripped and macro uses expanded. No code changes; the row&#x27;s count is the deliverable. — evidence: regress/artifacts/cpusweep_20260917/SWEEP_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/ spelling census</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seatauditgap</span><span class="ttl">seat-coverage audit</span><span class="tip">seat-coverage audit</span></span><span class="state st-in work">in work</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE GATE IS BUILT AND CONTROLLED; IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED YET. ***  Owner chose INSTALL/RETROACTIVE over the dated-cutoff form.  Built to that decision, with the rejected reasoning recorded IN THE FILE rather than quietly softened -- the carrier&#x27;s finding that a retro-failing rule gets switched off is the COST of the decision, not a veto on it.  TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS, both run: against the real ledger it EXITS 1 and names 34 rows; with the gaps synthetically closed it EXITS 0 and prints FIX_SEATS_PASS.  Ledger restored and the restore verified BY CONTENT (0 synthetic entries remaining, 488 rows), not by the backup&#x27;s existence.  *** A CLOBBER WAS AVERTED IN THE BUILD: the parked draft&#x27;s docstring named it `check_seat_coverage.py&#x27;, which ALREADY EXISTS and does a different job (per-seat answers-on-disk vs ledger coverage).  Shipping under that name would have destroyed it -- the same incident this session already recorded once.  Renamed, and the destination was checked for existence before writing. ***  WHY IT IS NOT WIRED: retroactive means it refuses EVERY commit until all 34 reconcile, including the campaign&#x27;s own records.  It lands at the END of the campaign so it goes in GREEN.  *** AND THE GATE&#x27;S OUTPUT IS THE CAMPAIGN&#x27;S WORKLIST, which is the useful part: the six rows panelled today are already down to MISSING: fable5 alone, so one Fable adjudication closes six of the 34 at once. *** — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_fix_seats.py (BUILT, NOT WIRED into precommit_check.sh)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">armthumbabortkill</span><span class="ttl">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING</span><span class="tip">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING — witness: none -- documented-only.  ATTEMPT RECORDED: a rung-3 probe was specified in full (regress/artifacts/armthumbabortkill/WITNESSSPEC_grok_20260917.txt) and then condemned against the tree across 13 holes (HOLES_grok_20260917.txt), two of which are unfixable as specified -- the t ifetch row is vacuous by construction, and the TLB flush the ARM control needs is the same flush that makes site 1227 unreachable.  Reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is built for a higher-ranked defect. — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the grok seat&#x27;s site-by-site triage of cpu_arm.c (the cpusexitsweep research row).  **FIVE SITES, ONE DEFECT: 1227, 1557, 1600, 1629, 1653.**  *** IT IS A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT, NOT MERELY A HALT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HIGH AND IS NOT PART OF THE CENSUS: on an MMU fault the abort is ALREADY DELIVERED and T is ALREADY CLEARED, so the `cpu-&gt;running = 0&#x27; that follows PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S OWN ABORT HANDLER FROM EVER RUNNING.  A guest that would have recovered instead stops. ***  DISPOSITION: option-set member 1 DELETE -- match the ARM load/store path.  **KEEP the `return 0&#x27;; do NOT fall through to `pc += 2&#x27;.**  IN-TREE PRECEDENT: #312 said of the ARM-mode analogue that &#x27;halting was never right&#x27;.  SELECTOR: a guest-supplied instruction or address in arm_cpu_interpret_thumb_SLOW, which is the PRODUCTION Thumb path -- cpu_dyntrans.c calls it whenever ARM_FLAG_T is set and ignores its return value -- so these are reachable by any Thumb guest taking a memory fault.  NOT YET WITNESSED: no probe has been built.  This is a READING by a file-reading seat, and the round that takes it owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires.  *** WITNESS DISPOSITION: DOCUMENTED-ONLY, WITH THE ATTEMPT RECORDED.  DO NOT BUILD `regress/arm_thumbabort_probe.py` FROM THE SPEC, AND DO NOT BUILD A PATCHED COPY IN THIS CAMPAIGN. ***  The lifecycle ask is satisfied in the form it was written for: a seat WAS asked to try a rung-3 probe, DID specify one, and then condemned its own spec against the tree.  So `none` here means &#x27;tried; the vehicle costs a new MMU-on ARM harness that this row does not outrank&#x27;, NOT &#x27;did not think to&#x27; -- which are different states and must not render the same. THIRTEEN HOLES, derived against source rather than restated.  **The two that end the question:** *** HOLE 3 -- THE `t ifetch` ROW (site 1227) IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION AND THE SPEC&#x27;S OWN SCORE IS THEREFORE A WRONG RECORD. ***  `X(bx)` sets T before `quick_pc_to_pointers_arm` short-circuits, so the BX itself does not translate the hole -- but the NEXT `run_instr` does: `arm_run_instr` calls the GENERIC `arm_pc_to_pointers` FIRST and checks T only afterwards, so the prefetch abort is taken with T already cleared and PC already at 0x0C, the ARM prefetch handler runs, and **site 1227 never executes**.  The row wants `SENT` (handler did not run); the handler DID run; so the row is GREEN on HEAD and GREEN post-fix.  It never reds -- and it inflates the spec&#x27;s claimed HEAD from 7/12 to 8/12.  A &#x27;Thumb ifetch&#x27; row built as specified is an ARM dyntrans prefetch-abort row wearing a Thumb name.  1227 is DISCLOSED, not rowed. *** HOLE 2 -- A GENUINE CONTRADICTION THE SPEC CANNOT SEE: the c8 flush that makes the `arm abt` control honest is the SAME flush that makes 1227 unreachable.  You cannot have both on one hole. ***  And the seat CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPECSET CLAIM here: stale VPH does NOT hide a Thumb abort (the five Thumb sites go through `memory_rw` -&gt; `translate_v2p` and never read `host_load`); it hides the ARM SIBLING CONTROL instead.  My own brief had stated this as a QUESTION rather than a fact, which is why no wrong record of mine needs correcting. THE REMAINING ELEVEN, kept because they are reusable if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built: (1) the TTB plant explodes three different ways -- 4096 `put w` of zeros onto already-zero anonymous mmap is 4096 pty round-trips against a 45 s wait, `put w` warms `host_load` because it does NOT use NO_EXCEPTIONS, and any `put w` after the M bit makes THE DEBUGGER take the abort; (4) the row set splits DATA {1600,1629,1653} / PREF {1557 only}, one probe, and PREF must NOT pin FAR/FSR because prefetch does not write them and a leftover FAR would lie; (5) `-T` is not host death, is IRRELEVANT at UNMAP=0x100000 under `-M 64`, and `-K` is redundant with `-V`; (6) LIVE must be a named constant, never &#x27;UNIQUE&#x27;s neighbour&#x27;, which plants next to the hole; (7) `mmu bit`/`ttb sent`/`thumb T` have NO observation channel as specified -- Thumb has no MRS; (8) rows 1-6 CANNOT share a session, because `arm abt` leaves the CPU in ABT32 at the handler -- three control processes, not one; (9) no `enc ok` for the planted Thumb halfwords, and a wrong halfword hits `:1808 default:` `running = 0` which LOOKS exactly like the abort defect on HEAD and false-reds POST; (10) the two vector `B` words are not in the spec (given: `0xEA0020FB` at 0x0C, `0xEA00213A` at 0x10); (11) after the M bit **testarm&#x27;s own LR stub is unmapped**, so `continue` or an oversized `step N` falls into it and takes a prefetch abort -- which is the POST colour of a PREF row; (12) flags `session()` is the wrong wrapper to copy even though a trailing dump IS legal for Thumb; (13) `return 0` is load-bearing -- DELETE only `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, because dropping the whole `if` body falls through to `cpu-&gt;pc += 2` on a PC `arm_exception` already set, making the vector 0x0E/0x12. **THE BILL IF BUILT AFTER REPAIRS: 7 sessions minimum, not 6, plus the first in-tree ARM MMU-on probe** -- no `regress/arm_*` probe enables the M bit today -- meaning CP15 c1/c2/c3/c8, a 1 MB section table, two abort handlers and architectural BX.  That is a round of its own spent on a FUNCTIONAL HALT (`cpu-&gt;running = 0` AFTER `arm_exception` already delivered and T already cleared; the abort handler never runs; the host does not die) while a PROCESS-DEATH row (`armmsrmodekill`) is unbuilt. **REOPEN CONDITION, NAMED SO THIS IS NOT A QUIET DROP:** reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built for a higher-ranked defect -- Thumb is then a RIDER on it, not its first customer. **THE HONEST COST, stated rather than glossed:** documented-only means the fix does not ship, so the five `cpu-&gt;running = 0` lines STAY.  The fix is known and is member 1 DELETE with `return 0` KEPT; it is unshippable under test-first without a witness. Seat&#x27;s own reservations, kept because they bound the answer: it would not claim without a run that 1557&#x27;s PC-relative load takes PREF rather than DATA on this binary, that `step N` under `-V` leaves DEST dumpable as SENT, or that `dump UNMAP` prints &#x27;(memory access failed)&#x27; rather than zeros a sloppy regex would read as UNIQUE.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pe