roved, not because a panel ran short.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CROSS-SPEC REVIEW OF ALL THREE WITNESS SPECS AS A SET -- none had been reviewed against the others, each was written in isolation.  VERDICT: DO NOT BUILD THE THUMB SPEC AS WRITTEN (&#x27;the TTB plant is a session bomb&#x27;).  BUILD ORDER: MSR first, then Alpha with one added stop, then Thumb. ***  IT CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPEC TWICE MORE.  (1) THE `printed()&#x27; INSTRUCTION WAS FALSE: it had told me to shun arm_bfx_width_probe.py because &#x27;the debugger prints r0 = 0x...&#x27;.  debugger_parse_expression returns PARSE_NUMBER, so `print &lt;reg&gt;&#x27; emits a BARE 0x... line; `reg&#x27; is the command with the prefix.  A builder who &#x27;fixed&#x27; the regex would score EVERY print as ABSENT -- and arm_flags_probe.py has NO printed() at all, so the advice was unfollowable.  Copy verbatim from lca_isalane_probe.py:397-399.  (2) THE MSR FAKE-GREEN MECHANISM WAS THE WRONG ONE: already-USR32 gives m=10 alive, which reds honestly (wrong-reason FAIL), NOT a kill-row green.  The real fake-green is encoding-nop / c-bit-off / MSR-never-stepped -&gt; still SVC32, alive -&gt; GREEN, because that IS the post-fix decline appearance.  Guarding USR32 would have guarded the path that reds.  NEW BUILD TRAP: DO NOT COPY arm_flags_probe.py&#x27;s session() wrapper -- it dumps ONCE AT THE END, and this guest calls exit(1), so the arming word must be dumped BETWEEN step 2 and the killing step.  That is also why MSR is built FIRST: it is the only one of the three that forces the mid-session dump, so Thumb inherits proven machinery.  ALPHA GAP: lcaisaphys states its stop in PROSE and emits no CONTROL=DEAD / RESULT=0/0, so a failed swpctx yields ~10/13 with defect rows GREEN that a RESULT grep will not refuse -- copy the ARM token.  ONE GUARD FOR ALL THREE: a positive un-fakeable observable that the bad path is ARMED, on the SAME PROCESS that issues the stimulus, before any defect row is read; red there is SETUP-FAILED.  Do NOT share an MMU-arming prologue between the ARM probes; share only pty/wait/send/sw()/-A.  `-T&#x27; is a silent coupler (turns a physical miss into host death); `-K&#x27; is redundant with `-V&#x27;.  All of this is in regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md, beside the spec rather than in this row, per the #490 lesson. — evidence: regress/artifacts/witnessspecset/CROSSREVIEW_grok_20260917.txt and CROSSREVIEW_brief_20260917.md; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — *** THE ORACLE IN THE WITNESS SPEC IS BACKWARDS IN THE DANGEROUS DIRECTION.  SINGLE-SEAT FINDING, UNCORROBORATED -- deepseek and minimax did not reach it, and that is stated rather than glossed. ***  The spec makes an armed kill row RED only on EOF **AND** the exact text `arm_load_register_bank: unimplemented mode 20&#x27;.  glm names the mutant the AND cannot see: A BUILD THAT STILL exit(1)s WITH A REWORDED MESSAGE -- and the packet itself spotlights the %i-vs-0x14 confusion, which makes &#x27;fix the format string, forget the exit&#x27; THE MOST PLAUSIBLE NEAR-MISS FIX.  That mutant gives EOF and no matching text, the AND never fires, THE ROW GOES GREEN, and the witness certifies a build where a guest MSR still kills the emulator.  *** THE AND GUARDS THE SAFE DIRECTION (a false-RED from an unrelated crash) AT THE COST OF A FALSE-GREEN, which is the wrong trade for a CERTIFICATION witness. ***  CORRECTION TO ADOPT: on an armed kill row, **EOF ALONE =&gt; RED; the text is the DIAGNOSIS, not half the trigger.**  Under that rule the feared cases still close: crash-elsewhere =&gt; RED (conservative, acceptable); print-without-exit =&gt; no death =&gt; GREEN, which is CORRECT because the defect IS the process death.  Buffering fear answered separately: exit() flushes stdio and a pty is line-buffered at the trailing newline, so the defect&#x27;s own death preserves its text; buffering only threatens SIGNAL deaths, i.e. mutant territory.  THREE MORE DEFECTS, all correctable: (C4) the spec SELF-CONTRADICTS -- &#x27;one fresh process per data row&#x27; AND &#x27;12 guest sessions&#x27; for 15 data rows, and 12 &lt; 15, so either three sessions host two rows (reintroducing the sharing hazard the rule exists to avoid) or the count is wrong; the row-&gt;session schedule is missing.  (Q5) if BOTH red rows are a single MSR #0x14 they are DUPLICATES -- a load-only fix turns both green and the witness certifies a build that still dies on the write OUT of the reserved mode.  (Q4) the SVC32 accept row is incoherent as stated: a same-bank switch cannot change SP, so &#x27;SP changed from boot to 0&#x27; is unsatisfiable there; and a fix repairing both LOAD arms while no-opping the SAVE arm passes every accept row, because nothing checks PRESERVATION -- a canary write to r13/r14 before the switch, verified after return, is owed for every mode but USR32.  FREE FINDING: the stimulus writes the whole control byte, and bits 6-7 of 0x14 are ZERO, so it UNMASKS IRQ AND FIQ; post-fix the guest sits in a reserved mode with interrupts enabled and any pending source takes an exception whose bank-save runs with the reserved mode current -- the store arm walks in ASYNCHRONOUSLY, making row timing nondeterministic.  It also HAND-DECODED 0xE321F014 independently and confirmed it, and confirmed 0x14 = 20 decimal. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/ADJUDICATION_glm_20260917.txt and ADJUDICATION_brief_20260917.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex=