3` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/PROBEATTACK_grok_20260917.txt; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromacceptdmanum</span><span class="ttl">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b)</span><span class="tip">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b) — witness: detector:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py -- the `accept dma&#x27; row.  *** THE TIGHTENED ORACLE IS UNCOMMITTED: HEAD still carries the loose substring.  The three-leg measurement is preserved in regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/acceptdma_20260917/ and the row closes when the probe is committed. *** — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the Fable late-review seat&#x27;s Q3 floor pass.  L2 by the letter of clause (b).  One-line repair named and DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED: the preserved artifact cannot verify it, because a probe log holds row output rather than the guest transcript, so the tightened literal must be proven against a RUN first.  Second clause-(b)-family gap in this detector in one day -- the class-closure amendment is engaged if a third arrives.  *** CLAUSE (c) ADJUDICATED AND DISCHARGED 2026-09-17.  NOT L2.  THE REPAIR MAY SHIP FROM THIS FLOOR QUESTION. ***  I had filed, in my own checkpoint, that this probe &quot;has no EXPECT_ROWS identity row -- clause (c) gap&quot;.  I measured that FALSE while writing the brief (the probe carries `live`, `devsig` and a `scan uniq` pinned by EQUALITY at 56), said so in the brief, and asked the sharper question instead -- and the seat then refuted TWO of the three carriers I had named.  **`live` is a RAM round-trip and `devsig` is the SH4 IPRD reset value, not a GDROM signature; neither discharges (c) against an emulator whose GDROM is gone.  The carriers that do the GDROM work are `impl ok` (CNTHI store/readback -- the row this file ALREADY added after measuring a value-only device row vacuous) and `scan uniq`.**  The project&#x27;s own CHANGELOG had already ruled `live` &quot;is a liveness control and CANNOT DO MORE&quot;, so my list repeated a mislabel the record had corrected once. **THE MEASURED ANSWER, per medium:** garbage BIN -&gt; every session dies, `live` wants 0x12345678 and gets &quot;died&quot;, `GDROM_DEF_CONTROL=DEAD`, result 0/62 or no `_RESULT=` line at all; comment-only product source -&gt; does not COMPILE (the `Mc_null` form); GDROM removed or emptied -&gt; `impl ok` and `scan uniq` red, DMA rows red with them.  **Not green on any medium clause (c) has ever been scored on.** **Q3, THE GATE PIN:** it is neither sufficient nor necessary, and the honest statement is both halves.  Not sufficient -- kill tables grep `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=n/n` without asserting `n==62`, and a gate can `gate_skip`.  Not necessary -- the probe fails a null medium on its OWN rows.  **And this SECTION does not skip a missing result: `gate_sh_rounding.sh:518` is `check &quot;gdrom_default probe completed&quot; &quot;no&quot; &quot;yes&quot;`, a FAIL.**  Verified here.  The 62-name list and the equality pin are detector-hygiene on the battery path, doing for this probe what `EXPECT_ROWS` does for the `check_min` family -- they are not the (c) discharge. **Q2&#x27;s DENOMINATOR CUT AGAINST MY WORRY:** `EXPECT_ROWS` is in 13 of 99 `regress/*.py` and the 13 are a CLUSTER -- the SH-4 family (gate uses `check_min`, so the probe needs its own stale-copy guard; `sh4_chcr_probe.py` says exactly that in its own words and caught 29-vs-27 where `check_min` would have passed) plus the NO-GUEST source-grep probes where `live`/`devsig` cannot exist.  Runtime-guest siblings carry none and all shipped above the floor. **NO REPAIR OWED, and the tempting one is refused with a reason:** a NEW identity row makes the score 63/63 against an EQUALITY pin at 62 and reds the gate unless the pin moves in the same commit -- do not manufacture that as a floor repair.  A hygiene self-pin (`if len(rows) != 62: n = 0` before the `_RESULT=` print) is available, moves no row, and is NOT owed. **ONE REAL SHAPE THE SEAT NAMED AND DID NOT DISMISS:** a STALE detector -- 58 rows, all green, reporting `58/58` -- WOULD look clean standalone.  That is not the medium clause (c) is scored on and is not what L2 is for; it is what the gate&#x27;s equality pin and the 62-name list exist to catch, which is why they are hygiene rather than redundancy.  Residuals filed as `gdromacceptdmadebt`.  *** RE-OPENED 2026-09-17: THIS ROW WAS `closed` ON A WITNESS CLAIM THAT IS TRUE OF NO COMMITTED TREE, AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THE PANEL ABOUT IT STATED THE OPPOSITE PREMISE. ***  Measured by the Opus 5 seat and reproduced here:   HEAD (9c91999)  `row(&quot;accept dma&quot;, &quot;yes&quot; if (&quot;Transfering&quot; in t and alive) ...` -- the LOOSE substring, i.e. the vacuous oracle this row exists to replace;   working tree     the TIGHTENED oracle, with the three-leg measurement in its own comments. So the repair **was applied** -- my brief asserted it was *&quot;NAMED AND DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED&quot;*, which is false -- and it is sitting UNCOMMITTED.  The `witness` field&#x27;s claim (*&quot;oracle tightened from substring presence to the COUNT&quot;*) describes the working tree only. **WHY THIS MAKES THE DISPOSITION WORSE RATHER THAN BETTER, in the seat&#x27;s own framing:** a floor clause-(b) breach is **L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND** -- and an uncommitted repair has not shipped.  A `git checkout` or `stash` loses it with nothing saying so, and the ledger has already recorded the obligation as discharged. **THE DISCHARGE IS CHEAP AND OWES NO NEW MEASUREMENT.**  The three legs are preserved and the seat read them: base with the tightened oracle `accept dma ok`; the `M_lenrev` mutant with the tightened oracle `SOLE RED`; the same mutant with the SHIPPED oracle `ok` -- **that third leg is the escape, and it is a real negative control with abort-guards in both directions.**  COMMIT THE PROBE AND THE ROW CLOSES. *The brief asked &quot;can a closure rest on a repair that was named and not applied?&quot;  The question was moot; the live one is whether a closure can rest on a repair that exists only in an uncommitted working tree, and the answer is no.  **A premise stated as fact in a brief came back refuted with a `git show` -- the third trial of the premise-audit opener and its third catch.**  This is also the CHECK THE SEAT RECOMMENDS TO EVERY FUTURE BATCH: for each closed row, is the thing the witness names present at HEAD, or only in your working tree?*  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *&quot;I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet&quot;* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open &quot;on packet-only review&quot;, which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file&#x27;s last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *&quot;row 9&#x27;s &#x27;not applied&#x27; and &#x27;unproven&#x27; descriptions are stale locally&quot;* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *&quot;an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.&quot;* *** THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *&quot;named and deliberately not applied&quot;*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5&#x27;s LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *&quot;&#x27;#481&#x27;s rows&#x27; is insufficient&quot;*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek&#x27;s HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *&quot;edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.&quot;*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *&quot;combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does&quot;* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *&quot;these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.&quot;*  *** F5 WITHDRAWN 2026-09-17: deepseek&#x27;s &#x27;hypothetical mutant&#x27; IS NOT CORROBORATION, and the Fable seat is right that my own rule scores it at ZERO. ***  I recorded that deepseek proposing *&quot;edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green&quot;* was striking because it described the real committed state without file access. **The brief handed it every element**: that the old oracle was the word `Transfering`, that the repair tightens it to the COUNT, and (falsely) that the repair was not applied.  A packet-fed seat answering the smallest-edit question from those three sentences produces that mutant BY CONSTRUCTION. *&quot;It could not have been copied from the tree&quot; is true and IRRELEVANT: it was copied from the packet.*  The standing rule -- score agreement only where a seat re-derived something the brief did NOT give it -- scores this at zero.  **The evidence for the breach is leg 3 of the preserved three-leg measurement (`probe_M_lenrev_SHIPPED.raw`, 62/62, `accept dma ok`), not deepseek.** *I found the coincidence striking and wrote it up as corroboration without asking what the packet had already told the seat -- which is the unanimity-is-worth-nothing rule landing on me, one level up.*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX&#x27;S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief&#x27;s own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. &lt;id&gt; (SEV, closed) -- &lt;title&gt;`: the packet, handed back as the seat&#x27;s verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: &quot;an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.&quot; ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex&#x27;s real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY &#x27;SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS&#x27; ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET&#x27;S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief&#x27;s S0 told the seats *&quot;an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction&quot;* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2&#x27;s condition (c), row 8&#x27;s figures, deepseek&#x27;s rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *&quot;the file-reading seats verified it&quot; is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5&#x27;s arithmetic, row 5&#x27;s unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm&#x27;s *&quot;nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire&quot;* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex&#x27;s row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into &quot;the file-reading seats said CLOSE&quot;.</span></span><span class="state st-in work">in work</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **KEEP-OPEN** (already `in work`).  `HEAD` is `&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`; the working tree is `&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot;`; `commits: []`; and **the artifact directory is UNTRACKED** -- *&quot;durable in location only&quot;*, and it must ship in the same commit as the probe. — evidence: fable adjudication 2026-09-17; regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/head_20260917/fable_probe_at_HEAD_20260917.out</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **KEEP-OPEN -- and it refuted the brief&#x27;s premise to get there.**  See the re-opening note above: the repair WAS applied and is UNCOMMITTED, so the witness claim is true of no committed tree. — evidence: _scratchpad/batch_closures_20260917.md; opus5 answer 2026-09-17; verified against HEAD</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **VERDICT: CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9 as recorded.  Row 9&#x27;s repair is applied and proven in the working tree, but has not shipped in committed HEAD.**  **KEEP-OPEN.** *&quot;The measured repair exists locally, but committed HEAD retains the word-only oracle and the supporting artifact directory&quot;* is untracked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **`gdromacceptdmanum`**: **KEEP-OPEN** — The brief asserts that a closure can rest on an unapplied repair; but at git `HEAD`, the tightened oracle is **NOT committed** (it exists only as an uncommitted working tree modification in [`regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1579`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gdrom_default_probe.py#L1579) and [`ledger.json:40672`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json#L40672) lists `&quot;commits&quot;: []`), leaving `HEAD` in breach of vacuity floor clause (b). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** | 9 | `gdromacceptdmanum` | **KEEP-OPEN** | Floor breach is *measured*. Closure is not: `commits` is empty, last commit that touched `gdrom_default_probe.py` is `#490`, HEAD still has the substring oracle. | — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/grok.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **`gdromacceptdmanum`: KEEP-OPEN.** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_kimi.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **gdromacceptdmanum — KEEP-OPEN.** READ: the packet states both that the oracle &quot;was tightened from substring presence to the COUNT&quot; and that &quot;the tightened literal&quot; was named and deliberately not applied. Both cannot be fully informative as written, and the one-line repair is never identified. Note the inversion the author may have missed: row 9 is the **only** one of rows 2/5/9 that satisfies all three S3 conditions (measured three-leg control, carrying row named, durable artifacts path) — its exposure is the applied/not-applied tension, not the OVERTAKEN conditions. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_glm.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. `gdromacceptdmanum` — KEEP-OPEN. READ: closure explicitly admits repair named and not applied; cannot be sound. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** | 9 gdromacceptdmanum | KEEP-OPEN | Repair named, not applied; closure rests on oracle tightening alone | — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CLAUSE (b) BREACH MEASURED AND REPAIRED.  THREE LEGS. ***  leg1 TIGHTENED oracle + base = 62/62 `accept dma&#x27; ok; leg2 TIGHTENED + M_lenrev = 61/62 SOLE RED; *** leg3 SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, `accept dma&#x27; GREEN -- THE TOTAL ESCAPE. ***  M_lenrev is one edit on a unique anchor: `(int) d-&gt;data_len, dst, length);&#x27; -&gt; `length, dst, length);&#x27;, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the pre-#462 defect at a FIXED SITE, which is exactly what clause (b) requires a detector to fail on.  WHY LEG 3 EXISTS: legs 1-2 prove the REPAIR WORKS and do NOT prove the SHIPPED oracle MISSES the mutant -- that was an inference from `&quot;Transfering&quot; in t&#x27; being substring-insensitive.  Clause (b) is a claim about the SHIPPED detector, so without leg 3 this was BELIEVED wearing CONFIRMED&#x27;s clothes.  Leg 3 carries positive controls BOTH ways: it aborts if the frozen shipped probe lacks the untightened oracle AND aborts if it already carries the tightened one (which would look like a clean run while measuring nothing).  WHY THE TIGHTENED ORACLE LIVED IN A FROZEN COPY UNTIL LEG 1 RAN: the literal had to be proven against the REAL transcript first; a wrong literal would have reddened the gate with no obvious cause.  Oracle-correction order run FORWARDS.  *** AND THE ARTIFACT COULD NOT HAVE SETTLED IT: probe_base.raw holds ROW OUTPUT, not the guest transcript -- grep for `Transfering [0-9]* bytes&#x27; returns ZERO on it while `accept dma&#x27; reads ok, because the tested string lives in the probe&#x27;s internal `t&#x27;.  A preserved probe log answers what the ROWS scored, never what the GUEST printed. ***  REPAIR IS ORACLE-ONLY: row count unchanged at 62, every row NAME identical (diffed against the pre-edit copy), so the gate pin stays 62 and the named-row list needs no edit.  DISCLOSED, NOT CLAIMED: the literal pins 408 because that is what THIS session&#x27;s staging produces; a future round changing the staged size must RE-DERIVE it rather than assume it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/acceptdma_20260917/VERDICT.md and logs/; sentinels KILLACCEPTDMA_DONE 16:50:19 and LEG3_DONE 17:07:48</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">untrackeddetectors</span><span class="ttl">regress/crime_width_probe.py and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py are UNTRACKED -- two shipped, measured detectors exist in no commit, and the checker that said so was reporting it as &#x27;no such file&#x27;</span><span class="tip">regress/crime_width_probe.py and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py are UNTRACKED -- two shipped, measured detectors exist in no commit, and the checker that said so was reporting it as &#x27;no such file&#x27; — note: crime_width_probe.py is the instrument behind today&#x27;s #529 ctl0c discharge (202/204); lca_isalane_probe.py is the 352-row #530 detector and the file the lcaisaphys witness spec copies its helpers from.  A fresh clone has the measurements and not the instruments.  check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s domain() is `git ls-files&#x27; (COMMITTED-OR-STAGED, per its own docstring) while its GHOST message said &#x27;regress/ has no such file&#x27; -- a wrong record that discredited a true finding; corrected the same day.  150 GHOST rows: 148 per-round artifact copies, exactly 2 top-level detectors.  *** CORRECTED SAME HOUR: the exposure is PROSPECTIVE, not present.  The COMMITTED gate_arm.sh has ZERO references to crime_width_probe (git show HEAD:regress/gate_arm.sh | grep -c) -- only the MODIFIED working copy invokes it a