ut; src/devices/dev_sgi_ip30.c:78,127,135,145</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — **RATIFY the reproduction, the fix direction, and the F13 silent-decline ruling** (verified in the working-tree diff).  *** DISSENT ON ONE DISPOSITION THE PANEL RAISED AND NOBODY CLOSED: `set_isr` KEEPS REPLACE SEMANTICS UNDER A DOCUMENT THAT SAYS SET-BITS, AND NO ROW CAN SEE IT. ***  codex raised it (*whether Set ISR replaces the value or sets selected bits ... the missing premise is the register&#x27;s write semantics*) and grok wanted the assignment declined outright; neither was disposed.  VERIFIED in this round&#x27;s OWN fetched warrant, `HEART_reference_20260917.txt`: *WRITE set_isr to set bits, WRITE clear_isr to clear bits, READ isr for status*.  VERIFIED in the diff: `case 0x10020: d-&gt;isr = idata;` (REPLACE) sits beside `case 0x10028: d-&gt;isr &amp;= ~idata;` (bitwise) -- **one register pair, one document, two semantics, and the asymmetry is the tell.**  Why no row sees it: `isr keep` seeds a ZERO ISR through the Set arm, where `=` and `|=` COINCIDE.  Settles in ONE session: `sd 0x1` then `sd 0x2` to `0x0ff10020`, then `ld 0x0ff10030` -- set-bits gives 0x3, replace gives 0x2, guest-visible through the one read heart.h defines.  Disposition: either `|=` with that row, or keep `=` relabelled **member 6 BACKED ACCEPT** with *replace is a fork choice, not heart.h&#x27;s* written where the label is.  **Never member 4** -- member 4 requires that the model already computes the right value, which is exactly what is unestablished.  TWO LIVE RECORDS DEFECTS: `CHANGELOG.md:54` still tabulates the READ arm as `3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE` while `:302` in the same block resolves F13 as member 2, and a reader meets the table first; and the probe is UNTRACKED under `artifacts/`, wired into no gate -- commit it under `regress/` with a gate section, or the closure is another `detector:` claim that nothing runs. — evidence: _scratchpad/fable_flagship_20260918.md (brief); flagship answer 2026-09-18</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says &quot;according to Linux/IP30&quot; three times, so the reference is Linux&#x27;s `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner&#x27;s approval on this project&#x27;s vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d-&gt;isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register&#x27;s store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR I