rints `Transfering 408 bytes` again and leg 2&#x27;s SOLE RED disappears.  The device comment&#x27;s own example is `0x3800`, so a guest that programs a length is ordinary, not exotic.  **The stronger session programs a DISTINCT length and pins BOTH halves of `Transfering %i bytes ... (guest asked for %i)`** -- that is the repair if this is ever bought, and it is a new session rather than an oracle edit. **(3) `accept byte` STILL USES THE SUBSTRING FORM.**  Verified at `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608`: `&quot;ran&quot; if &quot;Transfering&quot; in t else &quot;no&quot;`.  It is a DIFFERENT cell (the SUCCESS arm&#x27;s shadow-clear pair-oracle) and `accept dma` is the print site clause (b) was measured against, so this is not a (b) hole -- **but it is the old form left sitting beside the repair of it**, which is the shape a future reader mistakes for a decision. **(4) `nodata notx` counts `t.count(&quot;Transfering&quot;)`** -- harmless for a decline row wanting 0, and NOT a pin of the number. **(5) THE ROW STILL DOES NOT PROVE 408 BYTES MOVED.**  `accept data` (want `0x10` at the destination) remains the movement oracle; the tightened `accept dma` proves the LOG LINE carries the count.  The contract must not be read as claiming otherwise. DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed.  The stopping rule takes in-round only a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD; the wrong record in this family was the `EXPECT_ROWS`/clause-(c) conflation and it was corrected in place the same day.  (1) and (2) are contract-honesty items -- they matter exactly if the probe header&#x27;s &quot;prints data_len&quot; bullet is read as &quot;UNIQUELY data_len&quot;, which it should not be. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/FLOORC_grok_20260917.txt; regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">8259cmdhalt</span><span class="ttl">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep</span><span class="tip">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep — note: `dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm does `fatal(&quot;[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]&quot;)` and then `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- so the access &#x27;succeeds&#x27; while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu-&gt;running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat&#x27;s claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the `default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  **NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt&#x27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts. — evidence: src/devices/dev_8259.c:47,140,228-236,278; machine_cats.c -&gt; ali_m1543 -&gt; bus_isa_init</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunafbhalt</span><span class="ttl">dev_lunafb.c halts the CPU from four sites on ordinary framebuffer offsets, on a machine this project actually boots -- and the file had never been queued at all</span><span class="tip">dev_lunafb.c halts the CPU from four sites on ordinary framebuffer offsets, on a machine this project actually boots -- and the file had never been queued at all — note: `dev_lunafb.c` halts the CPU on ordinary framebuffer offsets: the `lunafb_a` and `lunafb_b` `default:` arms and a ROP-not-all-ones write all do `cpu-&gt;running = 0`.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:93`, `:160`, `:166`, `:185`. *** THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN QUEUED -- `lunafb` had ZERO mentions in the entire ledger before this row. ***  It is installed unconditionally from `DEVINIT(luna88k)`, and luna88k is a machine this project HAS a rig for and DOES boot, which is what separates it from the ip30 case (advertised but no in-tree boot image). REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED**: the seat&#x27;s claim is that `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` and `BMAP_BMSEL`, so every other offset in that window stops the CPU, and that a comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH is wrong while the `#define`s are right.  It also claims OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` uses BMSEL, which IS implemented, so the boot path is BELIEVED clear of the halt.  **None of that is re-verified here.**  FAULT (member 5) is architecturally available on M88K and `return 0` would deliver it -- today the halt runs first. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt&#x27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first. — evidence: src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101; src/devices/dev_lunafb.c:93,160,166,185</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunadefaultstop</span><span class="ttl">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id</span><span class="tip">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id — note: *** NAMED BY SEATS THREE TIMES AND NEVER QUEUED UNTIL NOW.  This is the records finding as much as the defect. ***  It was named `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the `lunaabsentcpu` pass-2/pass-3 panels with an explicit &#x27;file as a row&#x27;; the CHANGELOG still uses the name (**one** occurrence); the 2026-09-17 exithunt table named the same site with NO id; and the halt-class census named it again.  **Verified: `lunadefaultstop` was ABSENT from ledger.json and had ZERO occurrences in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.** THE DEFECT: `src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:923`, in the unimplemented-address arm -- `// Stop the emulation immediately.` then `cpu-&gt;running = 0; return 0;`.  **The device is ALWAYS INSTALLED** (no x11 conditional, unlike lunafb), and its window runs from 0x3ffffff0 to BMAP_START -- **roughly 1.76 GB**.  One supervisor store to any unimplemented offset in that range stops the CPU **on the machine this project actually boots**. **RANKED #1 of the guest-reachable halts** by the census, above `8259cmdhalt`, precisely because it is unconditional on a booted machine where `lunafbhalt` is X11-only.  Boot to `login:` is clear -- or the rig would not boot -- so the door is a probe, not the PROM path. CLASS: a FUNCTIONAL HALT, not a host death; the process lives and the strand stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to an `exit(` sweep AND to an `abort()` sweep, which is how the class survived three censuses.  *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass inside it is about **148 KB** -- four range handlers (FUSE_ROM 1 KB, NVRAM 0x1FDC, NVRAM_ADDR_88K2 0x1FDC byte-only, TRI_PORT_RAM ~128 KB) plus **34 exact switch addresses**.  But the set that MATTERS is three nested sets, not one: **(1) COLD DEBUGGER, TRANSLATION OFF** -- `APR_V` clear means VA==PA, which is exactly why `luna_intmask_probe.py` already reaches `0x65000000`.  Almost the whole window kills.  **This is the WITNESS population.**  **(2) AFTER THE MMU, THE PAGES OpenBSD ACTUALLY MAPS THAT THIS FILE DOES NOT IMPLEMENT** -- and this is the finding my row did not have.  `pmap_table.c` identity-maps **RW** inside this device&#x27;s window: `OBIO_TAS 0x61000000` (4 KB, nothing implemented), `SOFT_INT_FLAG0 0x6B000000` (4 KB, nothing), `RESET_CPU0 0x6D000000` (4 KB, of which only `+0x10` is serviced), `PIO0C`, and **`PC_BASE 0x90000000` -- 32 MB, nothing implemented**.  So the guest-kernel-reachable unimplemented set is **~32 MB, and 32 MB of it is ONE mapping**.  **(3) THIS IMAGE&#x27;S LOGIN PATH: EMPTY**, as my row said. **So &#x27;the #1 guest-reachable halt on a machine we boot&#x27; is true of (1) and (2) as CAPABILITY and false of (3) as TRAVELLED PATH** -- which is the distinction the ranking rests on and my row blurred.  *`PROM_SPACE` is the sharpest illustration of map-versus-defect: 256 KB of PROM, **one** word implemented, and that one word IS on the boot path.* **FAULT (member 5) BECOMES MECHANICALLY AVAILABLE IF THE HALT GOES, AND IS STILL NOT RIGHT.** Today the halt wins because `#220`&#x27;s latch in `memory_rw.c` -- `if (running_before_device_access &amp;&amp; !cpu-&gt;running) return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;` -- fires BEFORE the `res == 0` -&gt; `m88k_exception(DATA_ACCESS)` block is ever reached.  Delete the halt, keep `return 0`, and `#502`&#x27;s implemented DATA_ACCESS vector does fire.  Four reasons from the TREE not the menu: **(a) this file already models &#x27;illegal address&#x27; and it is NOT `return 0`** -- `case 0x3ffffff0` pokes `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR` into the data CMMU and `break`s, i.e. the access SUCCEEDS, thirty lines above the defect; **(b) the failed-device arm never sets PFSR** while OpenBSD&#x27;s `trap.c` READS `CMMU_PFSR_FAULT(tf_dpfsr)` -- so a naked `return 0` is a HALF-FAULT, an exception without the bus-error status the guest inspects; **(c) the same CPU and the same class were already adjudicated the other way** -- `pcc2defaultkill` `#501` declined unimplemented offsets with a latched `(once)` and `return 1`, and `dev_m8820x.c` `#433` says it in product comments, complain-and-continue, because `#220` assigned HALT to &#x27;the guest asked the machine to halt&#x27; and CONTINUE to &#x27;model gap&#x27;; **(d) FAULT on a MAPPED hole is a kernel PANIC, not a clean probe** -- OpenBSD `T_DATAFLT` with no `pcb_onfault` goes to `panictrap`, and TAS / RESET_CPU0 / PC_BASE are all mapped.  A driver that stores without `badaddr()` dies. *** AND THE WARNING THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE FIX: deleting the halt and leaving `return 0` would SILENTLY CHANGE EVERY UNIMPLEMENTED ACCESS from &#x27;stop the emulator&#x27; to &#x27;inject DATA_ACCESS&#x27;.  **That is a behaviour change to the guest, not a de-fatalisation** -- and it is precisely the kind of change the addition licence refuses without a measured delta. *** **THE SHIPPING TABLE (proposed, not ratified):** `RESET_CPU_ALL` **KEEPS THE HALT** -- OpenBSD `boot()` writes it to reboot, so the guest asked, which is `#220`/footbridge_reset; the `default:` arm gets **member 3**, latched announce plus **`break` and never an early `return`** because the common tail writes `odata` on reads; TAS / `RESET_CPU0..3` / `SOFT_INT_FLAG` / `PIO0C` are **member 7 FILED, not in-round**, being real registers whose implementation needs a measured delta or a transferred warrant; and FAULT stays **available-but-not-chosen** unless someone measures OpenBSD&#x27;s `badaddr()` against both a `return 0` and a `PFSR_BERROR` poke. *** `RESET_CPU_ALL` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT MAKES THE SIBLING TEST WORK: it proves this file ALREADY distinguishes a deliberate halt from an accidental one, so the `default:` arm is not &#x27;how this file does things&#x27; -- it is the outlier. ***</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass ins