bounded, but 16M-to-4GiB of host traffic inside a single guest access</span><span class="tip">four guest-controlled loop bounds at denial scale across sgi/ip32, dreamcast and mvme88k -- bounded, but 16M-to-4GiB of host traffic inside a single guest access — note: *** FOUR GUEST-CONTROLLED TRIP COUNTS LARGE ENOUGH TO DENY THE EMULATOR, ACROSS THREE MACHINES.  Bounded -- so not the `mapledmahang` class -- but bounded at denial scale. ***  Found by the signal-death hunt with an explicit denominator: `for (` 289, `while (` 60, `do {` 6, `for (;;)` 2 across `src/devices/*.c`. **(1) SGI MTE linear fill, `dev_sgi_re.c:1477`** -- `dstlen = dst1 - dst0 + 1` on uint32, so `dst1 &lt; dst0` UNDERFLOWS to ~2^32; the loop at `:1639-1677` subtracts at most 4096 per pass, giving ~2^20 passes of up to 4 KiB of `memory_rw` -- **roughly 4 GiB of traffic** from guest `mte_reg[]` values plus a CRIME_DE_START access.  Machine sgi/ip32. **(2) SGI MTE tiled fill/copy, `:1516-1574`** -- `for (y = y1; y != y2+dy; y += dy)` with 12-bit coordinates and no trip cap: **4096 x 4096 = 16,777,216** `horrible_getputpixel` calls, often two `memory_rw` each.  Same trigger. **(3) PVR / SH4 DMAC, `dev_pvr.c:171-204`** -- the hardware convention that TCR 0 means 16M is modelled faithfully (`:202-204`), so a guest programming DMAC ch2 with count 0 and writing PVR_MODE gets **16,777,216 transfers, one `memory_rw` each, inside one store**.  Machine dreamcast. **(4) osiop SCRIPTS MOVE, `dev_osiop.c:433`** -- `xfer_byte_count` is a 24-bit guest field, so ONE MOVE is up to **16,777,215** byte-at-a-time host accesses.  `MAX_SCRIPTS_PER_CHUNK` 256 caps how many MOVEs run per tick and does nothing about the size of one.  Machine mvme187/mvme88k. **WHY THIS IS ONE ROW AND NOT FOUR:** they are the same defect shape -- a guest-supplied count or coordinate pair used directly as a loop bound with no cap and no yield -- and the disposition is the same question for all four (cap, yield, or accept as faithful).  **(3) is the interesting one because the 16M IS the hardware contract**, so a cap there would be a deliberate divergence from the spec rather than a fix, and that is a different argument from the other three.  Filed as a class so the argument happens once.  *** THE HEAP-OVERFLOW CLASS IS CLOSED AS EMPTY, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR, AND THE SEAT REFUSED THE FINDING MY OWN BRIEF SUGGESTED. ***  I pointed it at `512 * count` on a 32-bit `int`, which overflows at count 8,388,608 and would give a SMALL allocation followed by a LARGE write -- a heap overflow, far worse than a denial.  **Measured: `count` is 1-256, so `512*256 = 131072` and the wrap is UNREACHABLE.**  Its own words: *&#x27;a manufactured WDC heap overflow would be a wrong record.&#x27;* **DENOMINATOR:** 130+ `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc`/`zeroed_alloc` calls in `src/devices/` and ~15 in `src/cpus/` production code, the large majority `malloc(sizeof(struct ..._data))` at DEVINIT or init-time name buffers -- host/config, not guest.  **16 guest-derived sizes across 10 files**, every one traced to its writer.  Result: **0 overflow, 15 capped or field-width bounded, 1 unbounded growth** (the mp IPI queue, filed as `mpipirealloc`). Each cap named rather than asserted: le is a 12-bit field plus a 12-1900 reject and a `size_t` aggregate check; 21143 an 11-bit field with an `int` sum clamped to 65536; gdrom a 16-bit `cnt` / 24-bit sector count with an explicit 64 KiB cap before `alloc_data()`; ps2 `0xffff * 16`; pvr Z 10-bit geometry gated by the 8 MiB VRAM check; pvr TA a doubling allocator; and `dev_fb.c` carries `#156`&#x27;s `size_t` overflow check **before** the malloc.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c 