REACHABILITY: **the test machines ALWAYS add it** -- `machine_test.c:148` and `:333` both `device_add(&quot;mp addr=... irq=...&quot;)`.  So this is not a device nobody installs. **SEVERITY LOW, RANKED BELOW `guestloopdenial`, and the reason is the shape rather than the size:** this is not a 2 GB one-shot allocation.  It is growth by accretion -- many writes, four bytes each -- so it is slower to matter than a tight loop and needs sustained guest cooperation.  It sits in the same family as `guestloopdenial` (the guest takes the host away without killing it) but arrives by a different road: **resident memory grows instead of a loop spinning.** *** NOTE WHAT `CHECK_ALLOCATION` DOES AND DOES NOT BUY HERE.  It turns a FAILED realloc into a clean `exit(1)`, which is why this is not a crash -- but it does nothing at all about a long run of SUCCESSFUL ones, which is the whole defect.  A guard against failure is not a guard against growth. ***  **ADJACENT, AND FLAGGED HONESTLY AS OUTSIDE THE DIRECTORIES THE HUNT WAS ASKED ABOUT:** `scsi_transfer_allocbuf()` in `src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c` is the malloc that SCSI controllers actually use, and devices pass guest lengths into it.  READ/WRITE is capped at **64 MiB** by `#163`&#x27;s `SCSI_MAX_XFER_BYTES` with a 64-bit multiply from `#133`.  But `dev_mb89352.c`&#x27;s `transfer_count` (a `size_t` fed from the 24-bit TCH/TCM/TCL register triple) is used as `want_len` for DATA OUT **and for the CDB**, giving a command buffer of up to **16,777,215 bytes** on a FIELD-WIDTH cap with no software cap behind it.  Machine: luna88k.  Recorded here rather than filed as its own row because it is a bound, not an overflow, and it belongs with whoever next touches mb89352 -- which `b120` already owns.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</s