#443 sh4pcicexit`** (*one guest LOAD ended the host, on 116 of 137 offsets*), and **`#493 wdcaltoffset`** (*a rung-3 witness for a live sev-high defect*). *** AND THE SHARP PART: `sh4bcr` AND `sh4pcicexit` ARE THE TWO ROUNDS THE VACUITY FLOOR WAS MINTED FROM. ***  The carrier cites them by name as its founding evidence -- *&quot;sh4bcr&#x27;s first detector passed a mutant reinstating the very host kill its round removed; sh4pcicexit&#x27;s passed the original kill verbatim&quot;* -- so **the most-cited exemplars in the whole rule set have no round block in the file a reader would go to.**  A future reader re-deriving the floor&#x27;s warrant finds the rule, finds the rounds named, and finds nothing in the CHANGELOG under either number. **THE GAP IS DIRECTIONAL AND IT IS THE INVERSE OF THE USUAL ONE.**  This session&#x27;s other records findings are all *written down but wrong*; this one is *shipped but not written down*, which no gate looks for -- section H checks seats were fired, G checks an answer was recorded, and `check_cited_artifacts.py` checks a cited path exists.  **Nothing asks whether a round that shipped has a block at all.** **DISPOSITION: recorded, NOT reconstructed.**  Writing seven retroactive round blocks from commit messages would manufacture records, which is worse than the gap -- so ONE consolidated *assessed-not-changed* entry names every round and its commits, and the reconstruction is filed for a round that can do it from the rounds&#x27; own artifacts. *The checker that would close this is a ledger-row-to-CHANGELOG-heading existence test, forward-only from a cutoff -- the `check_bugfile_sync.py` idiom.  FILED, not built: it is a tooling change owing two negative controls, and this file&#x27;s own rule is that such a change ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.*  DERIVER, because a count in a durable record names the command that made it and the tree it ran against (HEAD at the time of writing, working tree clean of CHANGELOG