e a latch at an unreachable site is the addition licence&#x27;s &#x27;cannot hurt&#x27;.  This changes the round&#x27;s SIZE and so the row title was corrected in the same edit.  (2) A NAIVE exit-grep OVER THIS FILE RETURNS 5 AND ALL FIVE ARE COMMENT TEXT -- the #223 comments that read &#x27;warn+ignore, do not exit() the host&#x27;.  THE FILE HAS ZERO LIVE exit().  I ran that grep first and it would have put a false premise -- five surviving host kills -- into a nine-seat packet, which is the class this project records as coming back CONFIRMED by seats that cannot check it.  Settled with the project&#x27;s OWN comment-aware tool, tools/pipeline/exit_census.py, which brief template section C0 names and which I found only by running C0&#x27;s third command; it reports 137 of 269 raw hits tree-wide (51%) are inside comments, and dev_8253.c is ABSENT from its live list of 26 files.  C0 CLEARANCE RUN RATHER THAN ASSUMED: last commit touching the file is af4884c (2026-08-20, #440, a different defect) and this row id appears 0 times in CHANGELOG.md, so the round has not shipped.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT open, NOT in work: no seat has answered, and marking a row in work on the strength of a staged brief is the gap-closes-itself shape. — evidence: _scratchpad/pit8253flood_pass1.md (462 lines, 22038 bytes); frozen source _scratchpad/dev_8253.frozen_20260917.c md5 a081564373cf81c2cf177b75ccbbc20d; tools/pipeline/exit_census.py output; recorded in _scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION.  Identity check passed: it named itself claude-fable-5-1 AND quoted CHECKPOINT.md&#x27;s first line verbatim, so prompt delivery and file access were proven rather than assumed.  *** IT OVERTURNED ONE OF MY SEVEN ACCEPTED DISPOSITIONS. ***  A5&#x27;s CONSEQUENCE -- &#x27;do not latch :342&#x27; -- is WRONG, because it confuses a BOUNDED ANNOUNCE with SILENCE.  A (once) latch announces the event on every boot that raises it; only repetition is dropped, and repetition carries no information once the defect is FILED.  Leaving :342 unlatched ends the round with a guest-drivable unbounded fatal() still in the file under a rationale that reads as principle and is not.  RULING: LATCH :342 AND REWRITE ITS TEXT, since &#x27;not yet implemented&#x27; implies a refusal when the write is ACCEPTED.  When the BCD fix later deletes the site, its row is re-pinned to ==0 under the oracle-correction ORDER -- re-pin, show red, then edit.  D1 RULED AGAINST MY LEAN, which is what I asked for: DELETE :198/:200, do not latch.  Its decisive evidence is something I had not found -- dev_pckbc.c:941-951 FAKES both downstream observables independently of the PIT, returning 0x21 with the refresh-toggle bit flipped by a function-static counter -- so there is NO guest-observable difference between modelling counters 1 and 2&#x27;s outputs and not modelling them.  That is member 4&#x27;s definition.  And the OpenBSD bell path that DOES program counter 2 has its other half, the 0x61 speaker-enable write, already ignored SILENTLY: announcing one half of an unmodelled bell is residue, not a house idiom.  D2 accepted -- it changes each row&#x27;s ORACLE rather than the row count, plus ONE combined-session row which is the only thing that catches a cross-site field typo.  *** AND IT NAMED A LIVE HAZARD I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: pit8253_latch_probe.py:321 keys COMPLAINT on the literal &#x27;huh? reading from counter&#x27; and row R2 wants ZERO of them after a control word, so ANY rewrite of the :270 text turns R2 into an absence row that CANNOT FAIL. ***  D3: witnessable, ORDER-constrained, not documented-only -- and it supplied the exact session order.  ORDER: pit8253flood STAYS FIRST and the demotion is NOT right.  Neither uncovered defect has a real-guest driver (codex searched the guest sources: no BCD, no counter-1 programming), severity is a tie at MED, and OWNER SEQUENCING BREAKS TIES.  SCOPE: five sites are ONE round, because two dispositions in one diff are still one fix design -- bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/; _scratchpad/pit8253_adjudication.md; _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Agent seat, FILE-READING and this roster&#x27;s measuring seat.  VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP AS BRIEFED.  *** ALONE AMONG NINE SEATS IT FOUND pit8253bcdbinary -- the BCD bit accepted at :310 and the count decoded as binary at :181-184, putting counter 0&#x27;s host timer ~4x off. ***  Also: :351/:354 are structurally unreachable (DEV_8253_LENGTH 4, one registration tree-wide, all four relative_addr values named); :198/:200 are member 4 ACCEPT not member 3, because nothing is declined and hz[1]/hz[2] have no consumer; member 7 IMPLEMENT is UNAVAILABLE for :200 since the tree has no audio backend, so the rejection is for availability rather than because a latch is right; FIVE separate named int fields by dev_wdc.c:93-102&#x27;s runtime-mutual-exclusivity discriminator; and clause (d) is applicable though de-escalation is not, with the smallest null-repair mutant named (d-&gt;warned_tone becomes static int warned_tone -- ONE WORD, and every emission-count row stays GREEN because a single-machine probe has one device instance). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — GPT-6 Astra at max, FILE-READING, 1,268,922 b.  Confirmed dev_wdc.c uses separate named int fields rather than a bitfield; ruled the de-escalation clause INAPPLICABLE and corrected the queue&#x27;s own sentence (it &#x27;reverses its premise&#x27;); INDEPENDENTLY found that the LSB-only arm installs no timer; supplied the witness-ORDERING constraint -- a signature control word DESTROYS the initial-format condition :204/:270 require, because mode_byte is shared across counters; and stated its own limit honestly: it built no witness, so that lifecycle step must not be recorded as completed. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 17,226 b, fired under PANEL_FIRE_AGY=1 per the 2026-09-15 owner directive.  VERDICT: GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Verified the frozen md5, the C0 clearance, the exit_census figures and the (once) census against source item by item.  *** IT FOUND A FALSE CLAIM IN MY BRIEF: section C2 said 26 files appear in exit_census&#x27;s live per-file list; the real figure is 44. ***  My deriver keyed on a lowercase-and-underscore filename pattern and so silently excluded EVERY filename containing a DIGIT -- dev_8253.c, lk201.c, dev_pcc2.c and fifteen more.  That is the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate failure this project already records, committed by me inside a brief that cites the rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 16,579 b.  VERDICT: NOT RATIFY member 3 for all seven.  Seven is a complete grep of the function, not seven guest-drivable floods, not seven declines, not seven IMPLEMENT stubs.  Two sites have no producer in the registered map; two more die after the first control word because mode_byte is shared; and the two named TODOs ACCEPT THE STORE FIRST.  Latch the reachable announces per site, do not decline them; de-escalation inapplicable. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — TRANSPORT: kimi-k3:cloud (the PACKET-FED substitute). the kimi SUBSTITUTE transport, PACKET-FED, 9,369 b -- fired ALONGSIDE the live CLI on this panel, so the kimi seat carries one file-reading and one packet-fed answer and they are recorded BY TRANSPORT as the doctrine requires.  *** IT FOUND pit8253lsbnotimer FROM THE INLINED FUNCTION ALONE: the LSB-only path never installs or updates any timer, so a guest programming RW=01 gets a silently dead counter with no warning at all. ***  Independently corroborated by codex, a file-reading seat, by an entirely different route. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — glm-5.3:cloud, PACKET-FED, 10,525 b.  *** IT PROVED MY C1 PREMISE FROM THE PACKET ALONE, AND MORE ELEGANTLY THAN MY FILE-READ DID: the brief&#x27;s own 18-emissions-per-session figure is itself proof that fatal() does not exit, because a killing fatal() caps the count at 1 and eighteen emissions require seventeen survivals. ***  It also found a detector defect no other seat raised: the emission-counting detector is ONE-SIDED, since a site silently