><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE GATE IS BUILT AND CONTROLLED; IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED YET. ***  Owner chose INSTALL/RETROACTIVE over the dated-cutoff form.  Built to that decision, with the rejected reasoning recorded IN THE FILE rather than quietly softened -- the carrier&#x27;s finding that a retro-failing rule gets switched off is the COST of the decision, not a veto on it.  TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS, both run: against the real ledger it EXITS 1 and names 34 rows; with the gaps synthetically closed it EXITS 0 and prints FIX_SEATS_PASS.  Ledger restored and the restore verified BY CONTENT (0 synthetic entries remaining, 488 rows), not by the backup&#x27;s existence.  *** A CLOBBER WAS AVERTED IN THE BUILD: the parked draft&#x27;s docstring named it `check_seat_coverage.py&#x27;, which ALREADY EXISTS and does a different job (per-seat answers-on-disk vs ledger coverage).  Shipping under that name would have destroyed it -- the same incident this session already recorded once.  Renamed, and the destination was checked for existence before writing. ***  WHY IT IS NOT WIRED: retroactive means it refuses EVERY commit until all 34 reconcile, including the campaign&#x27;s own records.  It lands at the END of the campaign so it goes in GREEN.  *** AND THE GATE&#x27;S OUTPUT IS THE CAMPAIGN&#x27;S WORKLIST, which is the useful part: the six rows panelled today are already down to MISSING: fable5 alone, so one Fable adjudication closes six of the 34 at once. *** — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_fix_seats.py (BUILT, NOT WIRED into precommit_check.sh)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">armthumbabortkill</span><span class="ttl">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING</span><span class="tip">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING — witness: none -- documented-only.  ATTEMPT RECORDED: a rung-3 probe was specified in full (regress/artifacts/armthumbabortkill/WITNESSSPEC_grok_20260917.txt) and then condemned against the tree across 13 holes (HOLES_grok_20260917.txt), two of which are unfixable as specified -- the t ifetch row is vacuous by construction, and the TLB flush the ARM control needs is the same flush that makes site 1227 unreachable.  Reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is built for a higher-ranked defect. — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the grok seat&#x27;s site-by-site triage of cpu_arm.c (the cpusexitsweep research row).  **FIVE SITES, ONE DEFECT: 1227, 1557, 1600, 1629, 1653.**  *** IT IS A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT, NOT MERELY A HALT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HIGH AND IS NOT PART OF THE CENSUS: on an MMU fault the abort is ALREADY DELIVERED and T is ALREADY CLEARED, so the `cpu-&gt;running = 0&#x27; that follows PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S OWN ABORT HANDLER FROM EVER RUNNING.  A guest that would have recovered instead stops. ***  DISPOSITION: option-set member 1 DELETE -- match the ARM load/store path.  **KEEP the `return 0&#x27;; do NOT fall through to `pc += 2&#x27;.**  IN-TREE PRECEDENT: #312 said of the ARM-mode analogue that &#x27;halting was never right&#x27;.  SELECTOR: a guest-supplied instruction or address in arm_cpu_interpret_thumb_SLOW, which is the PRODUCTION Thumb path -- cpu_dyntrans.c calls it whenever ARM_FLAG_T is set and ignores its return value -- so these are reachable by any Thumb guest taking a memory fault.  NOT YET WITNESSED: no probe has been built.  This is a READING by a file-reading seat, and the round that takes it owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires.  *** WITNESS DISPOSITION: DOCUMENTED-ONLY, WITH THE ATTEMPT RECORDED.  DO NOT BUILD `regress/arm_thumbabort_probe.py` FROM THE SPEC, AND DO NOT BUILD A PATCHED COPY IN THIS CAMPAIGN. ***  The lifecycle ask is satisfied in the form it was written for: a seat WAS asked to try a rung-3 probe, DID specify one, and then condemned its own spec against the tree.  So `none` here means &#x27;tried; the vehicle costs a new MMU-on ARM harness that this row does not outrank&#x27;, NOT &#x27;did not think to&#x27; -- which are different states and must not render the same. THIRTEEN HOLES, derived against source rather than restated.  **The two that end the question:** *** HOLE 3 -- THE `t ifetch` ROW (site 1227) IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION AND THE SPEC&#x27;S OWN SCORE IS THEREFORE A WRONG RECORD. ***  `X(bx)` sets T before `quick_pc_to_pointers_arm` short-circuits, so the BX itself does not translate the hole -- but the NEXT `run_instr` does: `arm_run_instr` calls the GENERIC `arm_pc_to_pointers` FIRST and checks T only afterwards, so the prefetch abort is taken with T already cleared and PC already at 0x0C, the ARM prefetch handler runs, and **site 1227 never executes**.  The row wants `SENT` (handler did not run); the handler DID run; so the row is GREEN on HEAD and GREEN post-fix.  It never reds -- and it inflates the spec&#x27;s claimed HEAD from 7/12 to 8/12.  A &#x27;Thumb ifetch&#x27; row built as specified is an ARM dyntrans prefetch-abort row wearing a Thumb name.  1227 is DISCLOSED, not rowed. *** HOLE 2 -- A GENUINE CONTRADICTION THE SPEC CANNOT SEE: the c8 flush that makes the `arm abt` control honest is the SAME flush that makes 1227 unreachable.  You cannot have both on one hole. ***  And the seat CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPECSET CLAIM here: stale VPH does NOT hide a Thumb abort (the five Thumb sites go through `memory_rw` -&gt; `translate_v2p` and never read `host_load`); it hides the ARM SIBLING CONTROL instead.  My own brief had stated this as a QUESTION rather than a fact, which is why no wrong record of mine needs correcting. THE REMAINING ELEVEN, kept because they are reusable if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built: (1) the TTB plant explodes three different ways -- 4096 `put w` of zeros onto already-zero anonymous mmap is 4096 pty round-trips against a 45 s wait, `put w` warms `host_load` because it does NOT use NO_EXCEPTIONS, and any `put w` after the M bit makes THE DEBUGGER take the abort; (4) the row set splits DATA {1600,1629,1653} / PREF {1557 only}, one probe, and PREF must NOT pin FAR/FSR because prefetch does not write them and a leftover FAR would lie; (5) `-T` is not host death, is IRRELEVANT at UNMAP=0x100000 under `-M 64`, and `-K` is redundant with `-V`; (6) LIVE must be a named constant, never &#x27;UNIQUE&#x27;s neighbour&#x27;, which plants next to the hole; (7) `mmu bit`/`ttb sent`/`thumb T` have NO observation channel as specified -- Thumb has no MRS; (8) rows 1-6 CANNOT share a session, because `arm abt` leaves the CPU in ABT32 at the handler -- three control processes, not one; (9) no `enc ok` for the planted Thumb halfwords, and a wrong halfword hits `:1808 default:` `running = 0` which LOOKS exactly like the abort defect on HEAD and false-reds POST; (10) the two vector `B` words are not in the spec (given: `0xEA0020FB` at 0x0C, `0xEA00213A` at 0x10); (11) after the M bit **testarm&#x27;s own LR stub is unmapped**, so `continue` or an oversized `step N` falls into it and takes a prefetch abort -- which is the POST colour of a PREF row; (12) flags `session()` is the wrong wrapper to copy even though a trailing dump IS legal for Thumb; (13) `return 0` is load-bearing -- DELETE only `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, because dropping the whole `if` body falls through to `cpu-&gt;pc += 2` on a PC `arm_exception` already set, making the vector 0x0E/0x12. **THE BILL IF BUILT AFTER REPAIRS: 7 sessions minimum, not 6, plus the first in-tree ARM MMU-on probe** -- no `regress/arm_*` probe enables the M bit today -- meaning CP15 c1/c2/c3/c8, a 1 MB section table, two abort handlers and architectural BX.  That is a round of its own spent on a FUNCTIONAL HALT (`cpu-&gt;running = 0` AFTER `arm_exception` already delivered and T already cleared; the abort handler never runs; the host does not die) while a PROCESS-DEATH row (`armmsrmodekill`) is unbuilt. **REOPEN CONDITION, NAMED SO THIS IS NOT A QUIET DROP:** reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built for a higher-ranked defect -- Thumb is then a RIDER on it, not its first customer. **THE HONEST COST, stated rather than glossed:** documented-only means the fix does not ship, so the five `cpu-&gt;running = 0` lines STAY.  The fix is known and is member 1 DELETE with `return 0` KEPT; it is unshippable under test-first without a witness. Seat&#x27;s own reservations, kept because they bound the answer: it would not claim without a run that 1557&#x27;s PC-relative load takes PREF rather than DATA on this binary, that `step N` under `-V` leaves DEST dumpable as SENT, or that `dump UNMAP` prints &#x27;(memory access failed)&#x27; rather than zeros a sloppy regex would read as UNIQUE.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span c