  1227 is DISCLOSED, not rowed. *** HOLE 2 -- A GENUINE CONTRADICTION THE SPEC CANNOT SEE: the c8 flush that makes the `arm abt` control honest is the SAME flush that makes 1227 unreachable.  You cannot have both on one hole. ***  And the seat CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPECSET CLAIM here: stale VPH does NOT hide a Thumb abort (the five Thumb sites go through `memory_rw` -&gt; `translate_v2p` and never read `host_load`); it hides the ARM SIBLING CONTROL instead.  My own brief had stated this as a QUESTION rather than a fact, which is why no wrong record of mine needs correcting. THE REMAINING ELEVEN, kept because they are reusable if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built: (1) the TTB plant explodes three different ways -- 4096 `put w` of zeros onto already-zero anonymous mmap is 4096 pty round-trips against a 45 s wait, `put w` warms `host_load` because it does NOT use NO_EXCEPTIONS, and any `put w` after the M bit makes THE DEBUGGER take the abort; (4) the row set splits DATA {1600,1629,1653} / PREF {1557 only}, one probe, and PREF must NOT pin FAR/FSR because prefetch does not write them and a leftover FAR would lie; (5) `-T` is not host death, is IRRELEVANT at UNMAP=0x100000 under `-M 64`, and `-K` is redundant with `-V`; (6) LIVE must be a named constant, never &#x27;UNIQUE&#x27;s neighbour&#x27;, which plants next to the hole; (7) `mmu bit`/`ttb sent`/`thumb T` have NO observation channel as specified -- Thumb has no MRS; (8) rows 1-6 CANNOT share a session, because `arm abt` leaves the CPU in ABT32 at the handler -- three control processes, not one; (9) no `enc ok` for the planted Thumb halfwords, and a wrong halfword hits `:1808 default:` `running = 0` which LOOKS exactly like the abort defect on HEAD and false-reds POST; (10) the two vector `B` words are not in the spec (given: `0xEA0020FB` at 0x0C, `0xEA00213A` at 0x10); (11) after the M bit **testarm&#x27;s own LR stub is unmapped**, so `continue` or an oversized `step N` falls into it and takes a prefetch abort -- which is the POST colour of a PREF row; (12) flags `session()` is the wrong wrapper to copy even though a trailing dump IS legal for Thumb; (13) `return 0` is load-bearing -- DELETE only `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, because dropping the whole `if` body falls through to `cpu-&gt;pc += 2` on a PC `arm_exception` already set, making the vector 0x0E/0x12. **THE BILL IF BUILT AFTER REPAIRS: 7 sessions minimum, not 6, plus the first in-tree ARM MMU-on probe** -- no `regress/arm_*` probe enables the M bit today -- meaning CP15 c1/c2/c3/c8, a 1 MB section table, two abort handlers and architectural BX.  That is a round of its own spent on a FUNCTIONAL HALT (`cpu-&gt;running = 0` AFTER `arm_exception` already delivered and T already cleared; the abort handler never runs; the host does not die) while a PROCESS-DEATH row (`armmsrmodekill`) is unbuilt. **REOPEN CONDITION, NAMED SO THIS IS NOT A QUIET DROP:** reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built for a higher-ranked defect -- Thumb is then a RIDER on it, not its first customer. **THE HONEST COST, stated rather than glossed:** documented-only means the fix does not ship, so the five `cpu-&gt;running = 0` lines STAY.  The fix is known and is member 1 DELETE with `return 0` KEPT; it is unshippable under test-first without a witness. Seat&#x27;s own reservations, kept because they bound the answer: it would not claim without a run that 1557&#x27;s PC-relative load takes PREF rather than DATA on this binary, that `step N` under `-V` leaves DEST dumpable as SENT, or that `dump UNMAP` prints &#x27;(memory access failed)&#x27; rather than zeros a sloppy regex would read as UNIQUE. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armthumbabortkill/HOLES_grok_20260917.txt, HOLES_brief_20260917.md, HOLES_grok_20260917.STOPPED_partial.txt; src/cpus/cpu_dyntrans.c:177-262</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CROSS-SPEC REVIEW OF ALL THREE WITNESS SPECS AS A SET -- none had been reviewed against the others, each was written in isolation.  VERDICT: DO NOT BUILD THE THUMB SPEC AS WRITTEN (&#x27;the TTB plant is a session bomb&#x27;).  BUILD ORDER: MSR first, then Alpha with one added stop, then Thumb. ***  IT CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPEC TWICE MORE.  (1) THE `printed()&#x27; INSTRUCTION WAS FALSE: it had told me to shun arm_bfx_width_probe.py because &#x27;the debugger prints r0 = 0x...&#x27;.  debugger_parse_expression returns PARSE_NUMBER, so `print &lt;reg&gt;&#x27; emits a BARE 0x... line; `reg&#x27; is the command with the prefix.  A builder who &#x27;fixed&#x27; the regex would score EVERY print as ABSENT -- and arm_flags_probe.py has NO printed() at all, so the advice was unfollowable.  Copy verbatim from lca_isalane_probe.py:397-399.  (2) THE MSR FAKE-GREEN MECHANISM WAS THE WRONG ONE: already-USR32 gives m=10 alive, which reds honestly (wrong-reason FAIL), NOT a kill-row green.  The real fake-green is encoding-nop / c-bit-off / MSR-never-stepped -&gt; still SVC32, alive -&gt; GREEN, because that IS the post-fix decline appearance.  Guarding USR32 would have guarded the path that reds.  NEW BUILD TRAP: DO NOT COPY arm_flags_probe.py&#x27;s session() wrapper -- it dumps ONCE AT THE END, and this guest calls exit(1), so the arming word must be dumped BETWEEN step 2 and the killing step.  That is also why MSR is built FIRST: it is the only one of the three that forces the mid-session dump, so Thumb inherits proven machinery.  ALPHA GAP: lcaisaphys states its stop in PROSE and emits no CONTROL=DEAD / RESULT=0/0, so a failed swpctx yields ~10/13 with defect rows GREEN that a RESULT grep will not refuse -- copy the ARM token.  ONE GUARD FOR ALL THREE: a positive un-fakeable observable that the bad path is ARMED, on the SAME PROCESS that issues the stimulus, before any defect row is read; red there is SETUP-FAILED.  Do NOT share an MMU-arming prologue between the ARM probes; share only pty/wait/send/sw()/-A.  `-T&#x27; is a silent coupler (turns a physical miss into host death); `-K&#x27; is redundant with `-V&#x27;.  All of this is in regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md, beside the spec rather than in this row, per the #490 lesson. — evidence: regress/artifacts/witnessspecset/CROSSREVIEW_grok_20260917.txt and CROSSREVIEW_brief_20260917.md; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">armmsrmodekill</span><span class="ttl">cpu_arm.c: ONE privileged guest MSR (MSR CPSR_c,#0x14 = 0xE321F014) ends the emulator PROCESS at arm_load_register_bank()&#x27;s default: -- a reserved M[4:0] is UNPREDICTABLE, not fatal</span><span class="tip">cpu_arm.c: ONE privileged guest MSR (MSR CPSR_c,#0x14 = 0xE321F014) ends the emulator PROCESS at arm_load_register_bank()&#x27;s default: -- a reserved M[4:0] is UNPREDICTABLE, not fatal — note: filed 2026-09-17 from a VOLUNTEERED grok side-finding (raised under the standing license while it was refuting a different brief), then CONFIRMED on a second firing by a full instruction-to-switch trace.  ONE ROUND, MEMBER 3 at BOTH default: arms.  *** SAVE (:559) IS NOT FIRST-HIT -- it runs on the OLD mode (SVC32, modelled) and returns; the process dies in LOAD (:606).  A LOAD-ONLY fix CREATES the Save kill, which is sibling parity and not a second row. ***  Member 1 DELETE is WRONG (leaves cpsr reserved and r[] on the previous bank -&gt; silent wrong-bank execution, then a second kill).  Member 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE on ARM but wrong: MSR is &#x27;Exceptions: None&#x27;.  THE DECLINE MUST SIT BEFORE SAVE, or roll cpsr back in Load -- callers write cpsr BETWEEN save and load.  RANKED ABOVE armthumbabortkill: process death vs running=0, cheaper witness, no fake-green hazard.  GATE 14&#x27;s 358 checks never feed the encoding.  *** REWRITE, NOT PATCH.  THE 16-ROW SPEC PLUS EIGHT AMENDMENTS DOES NOT CLOSE, AND THE SEAT THAT WROTE THE ORIGINAL SPEC RULED AGAINST ITS OWN WORK WHEN ASKED TO BE HARDER ON IT. ***  I asked plainly whether eight amendments on a 16-row spec meant the spec should be rewritten rather than patched, and named the `#392` shape as the thing I feared.  Answer: yes.  &#x27;A builder applying them in order will get 1+6 wrong and will either treat `save 2s` as a duplicate of `kill 15` or REPLACE `kill 15` with it, losing the `== 0x14` pin.&#x27;  *** AND THE TALLY ITSELF WAS A FROZEN NUMBER: it is 14/17 -&gt; 17/17 over THIRTEEN sessions, not 14/16 -&gt; 16/16 over twelve.  The original 12 was already false -- 16 scored minus `enc ok` minus the identity row minus one for live/pc/armed sharing S1 is 13, and the spec wrote 12. ***  THE VEHICLE STANDS and is unchanged: `-V -A -E testarm -M 64`, `0xE321F014`, member 3 at BOTH `default:` arms, one fresh process per scored row that executes a guest instruction, mid-stream dump.  The ROW LIST, the ORACLES and the SESSION ARITHMETIC are replaced. **ANSWER TO MY INTERACTION QUESTION 1+6, AND IT REFUTES MY FRAMING.**  I asked whether, once amendment 1 makes EOF-alone the kill trigger, the `enc ok` / `no cbit` / dump-before-MSR machinery becomes THE ONLY THING between the witness and a false green.  The answer is that those three are **NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT** -- each has a smallest defeat: dump-DEST proves MRS ran, not that the MSR word was planted or stepped; `no cbit` is greened by a decoder that never writes the mode field AT ALL, which greens every kill row too; and `enc ok` walking a constructor instead of the planted literal is the BFX incident repeating.  What actually closes false-green, all four required: (1) **POST want is `m=13 sp once`, not `m=13`** -- member 3 prints `(once)`, and encoding-nop, skipped-step and a silent member-2 all print nothing, so the latch must be IN the got string or its absence is a comment rather than a FAIL; (2) **poison `DEST+4` with 0xdeadbeef** and dump DEST+4, never DEST+0, so a never-fired post-MSR store reds; (3) **one `WORDS` dict**, with every `put w` drawn from it and a per-session plant-dump of the MSR slot -- this is the load-bearing anti-false-green that amendment 6 hoped `enc ok` was; (4) **named-red tally** -- HEAD must be EXACTLY the three named reds, because 17/17 on HEAD is not success. **ANSWER TO INTERACTION 3+5, AND AMENDMENT 3 IS HALF-RIGHT AND MISREADS ITS OWN CASE.**  The original two reds were `kill 14` and `kill 15` -- TWO LOAD HOLES catching the `== 0x14` special case -- **not** Load vs Save, which is what amendment 3 assumed.  So: SCORE `save 2s`, KEEP `kill 15`, do NOT replace one with the other.  The underlying worry stands: without a scored two-write row a Load-only skip-memcpy POSTs clean and still dies on the write OUT of reserved mode (16/17, `save 2s` red on the second MSR -- that is the point of the row). **AMENDMENT 5 IS TRUE OF THE ENCODING AND INERT FOR THIS PROBE, VERIFIED LOCALLY BY ME AND NOT TAKEN FROM THE SEAT:** `#0x14` does clear I and F, and testarm does hang cons/ether/rtc off `irqc` -- but ARM IRQ dispatch sits inside `if (!single_step) {` at `cpu_dyntrans.c:206` with the ARM arm at `:208-209`, and `-V` sets single_step; **`fiq_asserted` has ZERO occurrences across all of `src/`** and `ARM_FLAG_F` zero in `cpu_dyntrans.c`, so there is no ARM FIQ dispatch to enable; and debugger interaction `timer_stop()`s so `step N` runs no host-timer callback.  **The pin is STAY ON `step`, NEVER `continue`** -- a `continue` after a LANDED `#0x14` can take `arm_exception` and run Save on the reserved current mode, which is a free-run hazard and not a step hazard.  DO NOT change the filed stimulus to `#0xD4`. **BOTH KILL ARMS CONFIRMED AT HEAD BY READING:** `cpu_arm.c:559-561` `arm_save_register_bank: unimplemented mode %i` + `exit(1)`, and `:606-608` the same for `arm_load_register_bank`.  Two arms, two kills -- which is exactly why member 3 is owed at both and why `save 2s` is independent rather than a duplicate. **DROPPED FROM THE ORIGINAL SPEC:** EOF-AND-text as the kill trigger; the 12 sessions; &#x27;two reds because two default: arms&#x27;; POST `0xd3` on `kill 14` (member 3 refuses the MODE FIELD, so I|F stay clear at `0x13` -- record I|F, do not want them); already-USR32 as the fake-green mechanism; copy-flags-`session()`; copy-flags-`printed()` (arm_flags_probe.py has none).  Amendment 8 belongs on the ALPHA spec, not this one. **CLAUSE (e), DISCLOSED NOT ROWED:** register-form MSR (`X(msr)` copies rm into arg[0] and calls msr_imm); the 26-bit holes; the other 32-bit holes 0x16/18/19/1a/1c/1d/1e (`kill 14` + `kill 15` catch a ONE-VALUE special case, not a two-value one); SPSR-then-`MOVS pc`; free-run IRQ after a landed reserved write, pinned by stay-on-step. **BUILD FROM THE ARTIFACT, NOT FROM THIS NOTE** -- the complete 17-row table, the 13-session schedule, the CONTROL=DEAD set, the plant layouts and the tally live verbatim at `regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/WITNESSSPEC_REWRITE_grok_20260917.txt`.  Transcribing a row table into a ledger note is the frozen-number class waiting to happen. **ONE BUILD REQUIREMENT I FOUND MYSELF AND THE SPEC DOES NOT STATE:** the launch line uses `regress/arm_bfx_stub.bin`, which is on the NEVER-STAGE list and is UNTRACKED.  That is safe, and the reason matters -- **all 11 top-level committed probes referencing such a stub CREATE IT IF ABSENT** (e.g. `alpha_harness_probe.py:169-173`).  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` MUST carry the same self-create block, or it will be the one committed probe that cannot run on a fresh clone.  *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: &#x27;It runs.  It does not score.&#x27; ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- `debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start &amp; ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c n