its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/PROBEATTACK_grok_20260917.txt; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromacceptdmanum</span><span class="ttl">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b)</span><span class="tip">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b) — witness: detector:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py -- the `accept dma&#x27; row.  *** THE TIGHTENED ORACLE IS UNCOMMITTED: HEAD still carries the loose substring.  The three-leg measurement is preserved in regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/acceptdma_20260917/ and the row closes when the probe is committed. *** — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the Fable late-review seat&#x27;s Q3 floor pass.  L2 by the letter of clause (b).  One-line repair named and DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED: the preserved artifact cannot verify it, because a probe log holds row output rather than the guest transcript, so the tightened literal must be proven against a RUN first.  Second clause-(b)-family gap in this detector in one day -- the class-closure amendment is engaged if a third arrives.  *** CLAUSE (c) ADJUDICATED AND DISCHARGED 2026-09-17.  NOT L2.  THE REPAIR MAY SHIP FROM THIS FLOOR QUESTION. ***  I had filed, in my own checkpoint, that this probe &quot;has no EXPECT_ROWS identity row -- clause (c) gap&quot;.  I measured that FALSE while writing the brief (the probe carries `live`, `devsig` and a `scan uniq` pinned by EQUALITY at 56), said so in the brief, and asked the sharper question instead -- and the seat then refuted TWO of the three carriers I had named.  **`live` is a RAM round-trip and `devsig` is the SH4 IPRD reset value, not a GDROM signature; neither discharges (c) against an emulator whose GDROM is gone.  The carriers that do the GDROM work are `impl ok` (CNTHI store/readback -- the row this file ALREADY added after measuring a value-only device row vacuous) and `scan uniq`.**  The project&#x27;s own CHANGELOG had already ruled `live` &quot;is a liveness control and CANNOT DO MORE&quot;, so my list repeated a mislabel the record had corrected once. **THE MEASURED ANSWER, per medium:** garbage BIN -&gt; every session dies, `live` wants 0x12345678 and gets &quot;died&quot;, `GDROM_DEF_CONTROL=DEAD`, result 0/62 or no `_RESULT=` line at all; comment-only product source -&gt; does not COMPILE (the `Mc_null` form); GDROM removed or emptied -&gt; `impl ok` and `scan uniq` red, DMA rows red with them.  **Not green on any medium clause (c) has ever been scored on.** **Q3, THE GATE PIN:** it is neither sufficient nor necessary, and the honest statement is both halves.  Not sufficient -- kill tables grep `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=n/n` without asserting `n==62`, and a gate can `gate_skip`.  Not necessary -- the probe fails a null medium on its OWN rows.  **And this SECTION does not skip a missing result: `gate_sh_rounding.sh:518` is `check &quot;gdrom_default probe completed&quot; &quot;no&quot; &quot;yes&quot;`, a FAIL.**  Verified here.  The 62-name list and the equality pin are detector-hygiene on the battery path, doing for this probe what `EXPECT_ROWS` does for the `check_min` family -- they are not the (c) discharge. **Q2&#x27;s DENOMINATOR CUT AGAINST MY WORRY:** `EXPECT_ROWS` is in 13 of 99 `regress/*.py` and the 13 are a CLUSTER -- the SH-4 family (gate uses `check_min`, so the probe needs its own stale-copy guard; `sh4_chcr_probe.py` says exactly that in its own words and caught 29-vs-27 where `check_min` would have passed) plus the NO-GUEST source-grep probes where `live`/`devsig` cannot exist.  Runtime-guest siblings carry none and all shipped above the floor. **NO REPAIR OWED, and the tempting one is refused with a reason:** a NEW identity row makes the score 63/63 against an EQUALITY pin at 62 and reds the gate unless the pin moves in the same commit -- do not manufacture that as a floor repair.  A hygiene self-pin (`if len(rows) != 62: n = 0` before the `_RESULT=` print) is available, moves no row, and is NOT owed. **ONE REAL SHAPE THE SEAT NAMED AND DID NOT DISMISS:** a STALE detector -- 58 rows, all green, reporting `58/58` -- WOULD look clean standalone.  That is not the medium clause (c) is scored on and is not what L2 is for; it is what the gate&#x27;s equality pin and the 62-name list exist to catch, which is why they are hygiene rather than redundancy.  Residuals filed as `gdromacceptdmadebt`.  *** RE-OPENED 2026-09-17: THIS ROW WAS `closed` ON A WITNESS CLAIM THAT IS TRUE OF NO COMMITTED TREE, AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THE PANEL ABOUT IT STATED THE OPPOSITE PREMISE. ***  Measured by the Opus 5 seat and reproduced here:   HEAD (9c91999)  `row(&quot;accept dma&quot;, &quot;yes&quot; if (&quot;Transfering&quot; in t and alive) ...` -- the LOOSE substring, i.e. the vacuous oracle this row exists to replace;   working tree     the TIGHTENED oracle, with the three-leg measurement in its own comments. So the repair **was applied** -- my brief asserted it was *&quot;NAMED AND DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED&quot;*, which is false -- and it is sitting UNCOMMITTED.  The `witness` field&#x27;s claim (*&quot;oracle tightened from substring presence to the COUNT&quot;*) describes the working tree only. **WHY THIS MAKES THE DISPOSITION WORSE RATHER THAN BETTER, in the seat&#x27;s own framing:** a floor clause-(b) breach is **L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND** -- and an uncommitted repair has not shipped.  A `git checkout` or `stash` loses it with nothing saying so, and the ledger has already recorded the obligation as discharged. **THE DISCHARGE IS CHEAP AND OWES NO NEW MEASUREMENT.**  The three legs are preserved and the seat read them: base with the tightened oracle `accept dma ok`; the `M_lenrev` mutant with the tightened oracle `SOLE RED`; the same mutant with the SHIPPED oracle `ok` -- **that third leg is the escape, and it is a real negative control with abort-guards in both directions.**  COMMIT THE PROBE AND THE ROW CLOSES. *The brief asked &quot;can a closure rest on a repair that was named and not applied?&quot;  The question was moot; the live one is whether a closure can rest on a repair that exists only in an uncommitted working tree, and the answer is no.  **A premise stated as fact in a brief came back refuted with a `git show` -- the third trial of the premise-audit opener and its third catch.**  This is also the CHECK THE SEAT RECOMMENDS TO EVERY FUTURE BATCH: for each closed row, is the thing the witness names present at HEAD, or only in your working tree?*  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *&quot;I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet&quot;* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open &quot;on packet-only review&quot;, which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file&#x27;s last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *&quot;row 9&#x27;s &#x27;not applied&#x27; and &#x27;unproven&#x27; descriptions are stale locally&quot;* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *&quot;an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.&quot;* *** THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *&quot;named and deliberately not applied&quot;*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5&#x27;s LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *&quot;&#x27;#481&#x27;s rows&#x27; is insufficient&quot;*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek&#x27;s HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *&quot;edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.&quot;*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *&quot;combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does&quot;* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *&quot;these are coverage predictions, not newly run