INNED 8/18 (REPIN_prefix_20260917.out, the red), post-fix+RE-PINNED 18/18 (probe_FIX531_final_20260917.out).  An order proof is a claim about three runs of ONE instrument at one moment; re-running it on the grown form would destroy the only evidence that the re-pin preceded the code change.  The 18-row instrument is frozen at ip30_abort_probe.18row_20260917.frozen.py.  *** THE FLOOR IS NOT DISCHARGED BY 21/21: the two rows added for the L2 breach read `died&#x27; pre-fix because their seeding write aborts, which is the reproduction working and NOT a measured kill.  Kill table 1 (six mutants) is the discharge; PRETABLE.out was written before it ran and its mtime proves the order. *** — note: *** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, &quot;IP30&quot;, 30, &quot;ip30&quot;, &quot;octane&quot;, NULL)`. 0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d-&gt;isr = idata;` FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d-&gt;isr &amp;= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  `case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d-&gt;isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  `case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d-&gt;imask0 &amp; (1&lt;&lt;50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and `vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 9&#x27;s blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (&quot;No abort() is guest-reachable is FALSE&quot;), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month.  *** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, `IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- which IS the reproduction, since the house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30&#x27; with no `device_add&#x27; introduced to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd&#x27; DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd&#x27; alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE REPORTED `alive&#x27; FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF instead of waiting out its timeout, so the `spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single `waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB core, reports &#x27;still running&#x27;, and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample once** -- and `arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its witness will certify the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the same disagreement: my `devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit R10000, so a CORRECT device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 3 + MEMBER 4, AND THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY ARGUMENT. ***  I filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` occurs FIVE times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- with no definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` (`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  **Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the inference that it was cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil literals use a DELETED numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick&#x27;s `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating them onto `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 16550 breaks on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 7 on `lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP ROW** (implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  *Note the brief&#x27;s own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&amp;= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not &#x27;maybe an accept row&#x27; -- for the tick it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA&#x27;s assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` for unknown offsets; **this file&#x27;s OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file&#x27;s outliers, not the house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *&#x27;Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.&#x27;*  **The correct statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- `interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The distinction is not pedantic: &#x27;not available&#x27; would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4&#x27;S WORD &#x27;SEPARATE&#x27; IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling it &#x27;separate&#x27; is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi&#x27;s sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the missing test **is the read-kill&#x27;s entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** -- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d-&gt;isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store updates *&#x27;a register nothing reads for any purpose&#x27;*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`&#x27;s READ returns `d-&gt;isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive).  The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3&#x27;s warrant cited a PROM that &#x27;already talks to 0x10018&#x27; -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME&#x27;s *&#x27;one bit per site, never shared&#x27;* than from LCA&#x27;s two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write silence the timer-tick diagnostic forever** (grok, minimax, agy).  Disposition 7 holds AS A CENSUS but does NOT pick member 2 over member 3: the `fatal()` already exists, so de-fatalising KEEPS it and latches (grok). **STILL OPEN AND NOT INVENTED:** minimax says Q3 -- what a read at 0x10020 should RETURN post-fix -- **cannot be settled from the packet** and needs an IP30/HEART source, and there is no HEART manual under `_scratchpad/`.  That is an honest UNKNOWN and it outranks a confident guess; the round picks a disposition for Q3 with its reason stated, or defers the read behaviour.  **agy proposes a TICK ROW BY `step 70000`** -- `tickshift` 16 means 65536 cycles -- which would make the tick reachable WITHOUT free-run and **contradicts my &#x27;needs free-run&#x27; statement in this row&#x27;s witness limit.**  Not yet measured; carried as a named question rather than adopted.  *** THE TICK ROW IS BUILDABLE AND IS MEASURED: `step 1&#x27; KILLS THE HOST.  The codex/agy split is settled BY MEASUREMENT, and BOTH SEATS WERE HALF RIGHT WHILE THE DECISIVE FACT WAS NAMED BY NEITHER. ***  codex was right that stepping alone is insufficient -- the guard must be ARMED (measured: no store + 70000 steps -&gt; alive; bit 49 + 70000 steps -&gt; alive).  agy was right that free-run is NOT needed, but its stated reason -- a 65536-cycle period implying `step 70000&#x27; -- is not why the cheap row works.  **THE MECHANISM, verified by me in the source and not taken from the seat:** `machine_add_tickfunction&#x27; sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0&#x27; (`machine.c:354&#x27;) while `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 &lt;&lt; tickshift&#x27; (`:355&#x27;), and `machine_run()&#x27; decrements then fires while `&lt;= 0&#x27; (`:727&#x27;).  **So the FIRST `machine_run()&#x27; of any session drives it to -1 and fires EVERY tick function immediately** -- `step 1&#x27; executes the store and then runs the tick in that same `machine_run()&#x27;.  *** A THIRD FACT NEITHER SEAT NAMED, AND IT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: **BIT 50 CANNOT BE SET BY `sw&#x27;.** ***  `case 0x10000&#x27; does `d-&gt;imask0 = idata&#x27; with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)&#x27;, so a FOUR-BYTE store caps idata below 2^32 -- measured, `sw 0xffffffff&#x27; + 70000 steps -&gt; ALIVE.  Arming requires **`sd&#x27; = 0xFCE80000** (readback `ld&#x27; = 0xDC220000).  **`OP_SW_T0_A3&#x27; IS THE ONLY STORE HELPER IN `ip30_abort_probe.py&#x27;**, so a tick row copy-pasted from it would arm nothing, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH.  **PERIODICITY IS NOT A STARTUP ARTIFACT** -- the boundary was measured from TWO different warm offsets, both sides: warm=10 gives alive at N=65525 and DEAD at 65526; warm=100 gives alive at 65435 and DEAD at 65436.  Both put tick #2 at `machine_run&#x27; #65536 exactly, so ticks fire at MR #1, #65536, #131072.  **A guest arming bit 50 at ANY moment dies within 65536 instructions, with no access in flight.**  *The seat&#x27;s own first derivation said #65537 and its boundary run measured that false -- it recorded the measurement over its derivation, which is the right way round.*  STABILITY: run 1 and run 2 are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 13 scored rows (9/13, same four FAILs), `IP30TICK_CONTROL=OK` both times.  CONTROLS ALL GREEN, including the two that make the positive attributable: **`tick49 s70k&#x27;** (same store, same step count, bit 49 not 50 -&gt; alive) is the discriminator, and **`late s1&#x27;** (armed AFTER tick #1 was consumed, one step -&gt; alive) is what proves `tick50 s1&#x27; measured THE TICK and not something else about the first instruction.  **L4 APPLIED TO MY OWN PROBE:** `ip30_abort_probe.py`&#x27;s `acc imask&#x27; row claimed &quot;a handful of steps cannot reach it&quot;.  MEASURED FALSE.  The row is still safe, for two reasons it did not state (bit 18, and `sw&#x27; cannot set bit 50 anyway), and the comment now says so.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND:** codex&#x27;s constraint is DISCHARGEABLE rather than blocking -- the four-site closure is available because the tick row exists and is measured.  Artifacts at `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/`, all four carrying their completion sentinel, copied off the volatile scratch path the measuring seat itself flagged.  *** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says &quot;according to Linux/IP30&quot; three times, so the reference is Linux&#x27;s `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner&#x27;s approval on this project&#x27;s vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d-&gt;isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register&#x27;s store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR IT IN ITS OWN COMMENT: heart.h documents `isr` (0x10030) as READ-ONLY, and the fork implements a WRITE arm -- `/* Clear-on-write (TODO: is this correct?) */  d-&gt;isr &amp;= ~idata;`.  The cited document answers that TODO: NO. ***  Clearing goes through `clear_isr` at 0x10028, which this same file already implements, so the model has TWO clear paths where the architecture has ONE, and the fabricated one is the one carrying the self-doubting comment.  IN SCOPE for this round (same case labels, same function, same detector); it is a member 6 BAC