ll-through after a NON-EXITING fatal() -- the closure measured observable outputs and did not enumerate control flow after the de-fatalised call.  FILED with the F13 split. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260918_003753/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — MANUAL — Engaged directly with the disposition I flagged as least certain: *** &#x27;F10 SUSPECT -- author is right to be unsure.&#x27; ***  That is the packet&#x27;s §OWED item 4 working as intended: I named F10 as the ruling I least trusted because it leans on an invariant I had minted an hour earlier in response to the same seat&#x27;s finding, and the panel went straight at it.  Recorded as corroboration of the doubt rather than as a settled overturn -- kimi OVERTURNED F10&#x27;s warrant while allowing the deferral to stand, and that is the disposition carried. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260918_003753/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — *** THE OWED ROWS ARE WRITTEN (L1 CONSTRUCTION, NOT YET RUN). ***  Five mutants that scored 12/12 against the probe are now closed by construction: **`tick 50`** (closes M_tick_abort -- which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see -- and M_tick_swap); **`tick 49`**, its DISCRIMINATOR, so `tick 50` cannot pass for an unrelated reason and M_tick_bit49 cannot walk free; **`kill 20 wr` / `kill 28 wr`**, because the probe only ever LOADED at those offsets so an abort() kept inside the WRITE arm was invisible; **`isr keep`**, the `asicindexcase` row that closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the ANNOUNCE while `d-&gt;isr = idata` still runs on a load, which leaves the host ALIVE and the ISR CLOBBERED, worse than the defect being fixed; and **`kill 30 txt`**, a diagnosis sibling for the 0x10030 write arm so M_latch_silent_30 cannot delete its announce silently. *** THE TICK ROW REQUIRED A NEW OPCODE, AND THAT IS THE TRAP, NOT A DETAIL: the probe had ONLY `sw`, and a 4-byte store CANNOT SET imask0 BIT 50 because `case 0x10000` reads through `memory_readmax64`.  A tick row written with the existing helper would arm NOTHING, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***  Added `OP_SD_T0_A3` (0xFCE80000) and `OP_LD_V0_AT` (0xDC220000), and **DECODED BOTH FROM OUTSIDE THE CODE before trusting them** -- opcode 63 = sd with base $7/rt $8, opcode 55 = ld with base $1/rt $2 -- because this project&#x27;s hand-assembled-encoding trap is that a wrong constant is invisible when the probe reads the same constant. **`isr keep` READS `died` PRE-FIX AND THAT IS CORRECT**: its seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts on the committed build.  That is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure, and the row comment says so. **NOT YET RUN** -- five grok seats are loading the machine and this probe&#x27;s settle/spin are LOAD-SENSITIVE oracles.  The new rows are CONSTRUCTED, not MEASURED, and the pre-fix run owes a re-run with this instrument (a comparison owes ONE instrument). — evidence: regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py (tick 50, tick 49, kill 20 wr, kill 28 wr, isr keep, kill 30 txt)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** KILL TABLE 5: THE CHAIN CLOSES.  EVERY PREDICTION LANDED, INCLUDING ONE PRE-REGISTERED AFTER PRETABLE5 WAS WRITTEN. ***  base 33/33 | V2_assigntick 32/33 (xbit rev2 -- THE ESCAPE CLOSED; it swept 32/32 clean before) | V8_len4 33/33 ESCAPED AS PREDICTED, now measured at the CORRECT cell | V1_assign20wr 32/33 (xbit rev ALONE).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against the table&#x27;s 04:30:25.  *** THE DENOMINATOR, because a bare zero against a wrong enumeration does not close a chain -- this round supplied that counter-example itself at table 2. ***  TEN CELLS: {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} + {2 collision directions}.  Six closed before table 4; tables 4 and 5 killed the remaining four (V1/V2 the two collision directions, V4/V5 the two remaining latch-sets, V6 the tick latch-set).  TWO cells are DISCLOSED WITH A RULING and BOTH WERE MEASURED TO ESCAPE -- the counter (fix-independent, fable5) and the width axis at 0x10020 (outside the floor by the pcc2widthkills bound).  ZERO CELLS OUTSIDE THE ENUMERATION.  *** That is the fable5 fork&#x27;s FIRST branch, so the chain CLOSES and the escape hatch -- drop the latch axis wholesale to disclosed debt -- REMAINS UNSPENT. ***  A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION THAT HELD, recorded before the result arrived: PRETABLE5 said V1 kills via `xbit rev&#x27; but did not say whether `xbit rev2&#x27; would ALSO red.  I derived beforehand that it would not -- under V1 the ISR assignment clobbers nothing because TICK was never set, the tick then sets its own bit, and the second ISR access is correctly silent.  MEASURED: `xbit rev&#x27; ALONE.  *** So the two collision rows separate BY DIRECTION, which is stronger than either row firing on both: the signature identifies WHICH clobber, not merely that one happened. ***  V1 at 32/33 (was 31/32 with one row red in table 4) also discharges the #462 regression check: adding `xbit rev2&#x27; did not perturb it.  That claim was REASONED in PRETABLE5 and is now MEASURED.  CONTRACT UPGRADED: the width disclosure now reads MEASURED TO ESCAPE rather than &#x27;not swept&#x27;.  *** A disclosure backed by a MEASUREMENT tells a reader which edit walks free; one backed by an OMISSION only tells them nobody looked. ***  The incidental finding from the mis-built mutant is kept in the contract too: this detector DOES catch a width narrowing at imask0, with a four-row signature -- coverage on an arm the round never enumerated. — evidence: regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/killtable5_20260918/ (base, V1, V2, V8 .raw); PRETABLE5.out</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mvme187memcoob</span><span class="ttl">dev_mvme187.c:68 indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest offset of up to 255 before the switch, so offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OOB read -- today masked by the default arm&#x27;s exit(1), which is the asicindexcase shape waiting for a de-fatalisation</span><span class="tip">dev_mvme187.c:68 indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest offset of up to 255 before the switch, so offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OOB read -- today masked by the default arm&#x27;s exit(1), which is the asicindexcase shape waiting for a de-fatalisation — note: `dev_mvme187.c:68` indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest-supplied offset of up to 255, **BEFORE the switch and unconditionally on every access including reads**:  `odata = ((uint8_t*)&amp;d-&gt;memcreg)[relative_addr];`  The device is registered with length **0x200** at `:127-129`, and `:63-66` folds the high half down (`if (relative_addr &amp; 0x100) { controller = 1; relative_addr &amp;= ~0x100; }`), so the index domain is 0..255.  **sizeof(struct memcreg) is 124** -- DERIVED LOCALLY by counting the 62 `volatile u_char` declarations in `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_memcreg.h` (31 registers, each followed by a 3-byte `xxN[3]` pad), which independently reproduces the hunting seat&#x27;s figure.  **So offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OUT-OF-BOUNDS READ past the struct**, into whatever follows it inside the malloc&#x27;d `struct mvme187_data`. REACHABILITY: `machine_mvme88k.c:88` `device_add(machine, &quot;mvme187&quot;)` -- note this is `machine_mvme88k.c`, NOT `machine_m88k.c`, which the hunting seat named loosely and where my first grep found nothing; I corrected it by locating the constructor rather than by trusting the citation. *** THE REASON THIS IS A RIDER AND NOT A SECOND KILL: the `default:` arm already `exit(1)`s, so TODAY THE OOB VALUE NEVER ESCAPES TO THE GUEST -- the read happens, the process dies, and the kill is already inside `exitunowned76`. ***  **The exposure is what a DE-FATALISATION of that arm would create**, and it is the `asicindexcase` shape exactly: the `exit(1)` is doing duty as the bounds check, so removing the kill without clamping the index converts a host death into a SILENT heap over-read served to the guest as register data.  A round that de-fatalises this file owes the clamp IN THE SAME DIFF -- a bound, not a `case`.  Filed MED so it is queued BEFORE the de-fatalisation round rather than discovered inside it.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td clas