length register -- plus `accept byte` still carries the old substring form — note: *** COVERAGE DEBT ON THE CLAUSE-(b) REPAIR THAT SHIPPED 2026-09-17.  NOT A FLOOR BREACH -- the clause-(c) question was adjudicated the same day and came back DISCHARGED, and none of these re-opens clause (b) as measured. ***  The repair tightened `accept dma`&#x27;s oracle from SUBSTRING PRESENCE (`&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`) to COUNT-BEARING (`&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot; in t`), and three legs proved the breach was real (leg 3: SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, the escape).  What the tightened form still cannot see, enumerated by the reviewing seat and verified here: **(1) `d-&gt;data_len` -&gt; `d-&gt;cnt` or a hardcoded `408`.**  On this staging those three are the SAME VALUE -- TOC `case 0x14` accepts only `d-&gt;cnt != 408` as the decline and `alloc_data()` does `d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt` -- so the mutant still prints `Transfering 408 bytes` and BOTH the old and new oracles stay green.  The oracle distinguishes that cluster from `length` (`dma_reg[+0x08]`), which this session never writes, and not from its own members. **(2) THE KILL DEPENDS ON LEAVING THE DMA LENGTH REGISTER UNWRITTEN, and that is the sharpest one.**  Write `+0x08 = 408` and `length == data_len`, so `M_lenrev` prints `Transfering 408 bytes` again and leg 2&#x27;s SOLE RED disappears.  The device comment&#x27;s own example is `0x3800`, so a guest that programs a length is ordinary, not exotic.  **The stronger session programs a DISTINCT length and pins BOTH halves of `Transfering %i bytes ... (guest asked for %i)`** -- that is the repair if this is ever bought, and it is a new session rather than an oracle edit. **(3) `accept byte` STILL USES THE SUBSTRING FORM.**  Verified at `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608`: `&quot;ran&quot; if &quot;Transfering&quot; in t else &quot;no&quot;`.  It is a DIFFERENT cell (the SUCCESS arm&#x27;s shadow-clear pair-oracle) and `accept dma` is the print site clause (b) was measured against, so this is not a (b) hole -- **but it is the old form left sitting beside the repair of it**, which is the shape a future reader mistakes for a decision. **(4) `nodata notx` counts `t.count(&quot;Transfering&quot;)`** -- harmless for a decline row wanting 0, and NOT a pin of the number. **(5) THE ROW STILL DOES NOT PROVE 408 BYTES MOVED.**  `accept data` (want `0x10` at the destination) remains the movement oracle; the tightened `accept dma` proves the LOG LINE carries the count.  The contract must not be read as claiming otherwise. DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed.  The stopping rule takes in-round only a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD; the wrong record in this family was the `EXPECT_ROWS`/clause-(c) conflation and it was corrected in place the same day.  (1) and (2) are contract-honesty items -- they matter exactly if the probe header&#x27;s &quot;prints data_len&quot; bullet is read as &quot;UNIQUELY data_len&quot;, which it should not be.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** COVERAGE DEBT ON THE CLAUSE-(b) REPAIR THAT SHIPPED 2026-09-17.  NOT A FLOOR BREACH -- the clause-(c) question was adjudicated the same day and came back DISCHARGED, and none of these re-opens clause (b) as measured. ***  The repair tightened `accept dma`&#x27;s oracle from SUBSTRING PRESENCE (`&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`) to COUNT-BEARING (`&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot; in t`), and three legs proved the breach was real (leg 3: SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, the escape).  What the tightened form still cannot see, enumerated by the reviewing seat and verified here: **(1) `d-&gt;data_len` -&gt; `d-&gt;cnt` or a hardcoded `408`.**  On this staging those three are the SAME VALUE -- TOC `case 0x14` accepts only `d-&gt;cnt != 408` as the decline and `alloc_data()` does `d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt` -- so the mutant still prints `Transfering 408 bytes` and BOTH the old and new oracles stay green.  The oracle distinguishes that cluster from `length` (`dma_reg[+0x08]`), which this session never writes, and not from its own members. **(2) THE KILL DEPENDS ON LEAVING THE DMA LENGTH REGISTER UNWRITTEN, and that is the sharpest one.**  Write `+0x08 = 408` and `length == data_len`, so `M_lenrev` prints `Transfering 408 bytes` again and leg 2&#x27;s SOLE RED disappears.  The device comment&#x27;s own example is `0x3800`, so a guest that programs a length is ordinary, not exotic.  **The stronger session programs a DISTINCT length and pins BOTH halves of `Transfering %i bytes ... (guest asked for %i)`** -- that is the repair if this is ever bought, and it is a new session rather than an oracle edit. **(3) `accept byte` STILL USES THE SUBSTRING FORM.**  Verified at `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608`: `&quot;ran&quot; if &quot;Transfering&quot; in t else &quot;no&quot;`.  It is a DIFFERENT cell (the SUCCESS arm&#x27;s shadow-clear pair-oracle) and `accept dma` is the print site clause (b) was measured against, so this is not a (b) hole -- **but it is the old form left sitting beside the repair of it**, which is the shape a future reader mistakes for a decision. **(4) `nodata notx` counts `t.count(&quot;Transfering&quot;)`** -- harmless for a decline row wanting 0, and NOT a pin of the number. **(5) THE ROW STILL DOES NOT PROVE 408 BYTES MOVED.**  `accept data` (want `0x10` at the destination) remains the movement oracle; the tightened `accept dma` proves the LOG LINE carries the count.  The contract must not be read as claiming otherwise. DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed.  The stopping rule takes in-round only a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD; the wrong record in this family was the `EXPECT_ROWS`/clause-(c) conflation and it was corrected in place the same day.  (1) and (2) are contract-honesty items -- they matter exactly if the probe header&#x27;s &quot;prints data_len&quot; bullet is read as &quot;UNIQUELY data_len&quot;, which it should not be. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/FLOORC_grok_20260917.txt; regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">8259cmdhalt</span><span class="ttl">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep</span><span class="tip">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep — note: `dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm does `fatal(&quot;[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]&quot;)` and then `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- so the access &#x27;succeeds&#x27; while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu-&gt;running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat&#x27;s claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the `default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  **NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt&#x27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts. — evidence: src/devices/dev_8259.c:47,140,228-236,278; machine_cats.c -&gt; ali_m1543 -&gt; bus_isa_init</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·