27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first. — evidence: src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101; src/devices/dev_lunafb.c:93,160,166,185</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunadefaultstop</span><span class="ttl">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id</span><span class="tip">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id — note: *** NAMED BY SEATS THREE TIMES AND NEVER QUEUED UNTIL NOW.  This is the records finding as much as the defect. ***  It was named `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the `lunaabsentcpu` pass-2/pass-3 panels with an explicit &#x27;file as a row&#x27;; the CHANGELOG still uses the name (**one** occurrence); the 2026-09-17 exithunt table named the same site with NO id; and the halt-class census named it again.  **Verified: `lunadefaultstop` was ABSENT from ledger.json and had ZERO occurrences in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.** THE DEFECT: `src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:923`, in the unimplemented-address arm -- `// Stop the emulation immediately.` then `cpu-&gt;running = 0; return 0;`.  **The device is ALWAYS INSTALLED** (no x11 conditional, unlike lunafb), and its window runs from 0x3ffffff0 to BMAP_START -- **roughly 1.76 GB**.  One supervisor store to any unimplemented offset in that range stops the CPU **on the machine this project actually boots**. **RANKED #1 of the guest-reachable halts** by the census, above `8259cmdhalt`, precisely because it is unconditional on a booted machine where `lunafbhalt` is X11-only.  Boot to `login:` is clear -- or the rig would not boot -- so the door is a probe, not the PROM path. CLASS: a FUNCTIONAL HALT, not a host death; the process lives and the strand stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to an `exit(` sweep AND to an `abort()` sweep, which is how the class survived three censuses.  *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass inside it is about **148 KB** -- four range handlers (FUSE_ROM 1 KB, NVRAM 0x1FDC, NVRAM_ADDR_88K2 0x1FDC byte-only, TRI_PORT_RAM ~128 KB) plus **34 exact switch addresses**.  But the set that MATTERS is three nested sets, not one: **(1) COLD DEBUGGER, TRANSLATION OFF** -- `APR_V` clear means VA==PA, which is exactly why `luna_intmask_probe.py` already reaches `0x65000000`.  Almost the whole window kills.  **This is the WITNESS population.**  **(2) AFTER THE MMU, THE PAGES OpenBSD ACTUALLY MAPS THAT THIS FILE DOES NOT IMPLEMENT** -- and this is the finding my row did not have.  `pmap_table.c` identity-maps **RW** inside this device&#x27;s window: `OBIO_TAS 0x61000000` (4 KB, nothing implemented), `SOFT_INT_FLAG0 0x6B000000` (4 KB, nothing), `RESET_CPU0 0x6D000000` (4 KB, of which only `+0x10` is serviced), `PIO0C`, and **`PC_BASE 0x90000000` -- 32 MB, nothing implemented**.  So the guest-kernel-reachable unimplemented set is **~32 MB, and 32 MB of it is ONE mapping**.  **(3) THIS IMAGE&#x27;S LOGIN PATH: EMPTY**, as my row said. **So &#x27;the #1 guest-reachable halt on a machine we boot&#x27; is true of (1) and (2) as CAPABILITY and false of (3) as TRAVELLED PATH** -- which is the distinction the ranking rests on and my row blurred.  *`PROM_SPACE` is the sharpest illustration of map-versus-defect: 256 KB of PROM, **one** word implemented, and that one word IS on the boot path.* **FAULT (member 5) BECOMES MECHANICALLY AVAILABLE IF THE HALT GOES, AND IS STILL NOT RIGHT.** Today the halt wins because `#220`&#x27;s latch in `memory_rw.c` -- `if (running_before_device_access &amp;&amp; !cpu-&gt;running) return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;` -- fires BEFORE the `res == 0` -&gt; `m88k_exception(DATA_ACCESS)` block is ever reached.  Delete the halt, keep `return 0`, and `#502`&#x27;s implemented DATA_ACCESS vector does fire.  Four reasons from the TREE not the menu: **(a) this file already models &#x27;illegal address&#x27; and it is NOT `return 0`** -- `case 0x3ffffff0` pokes `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR` into the data CMMU and `break`s, i.e. the access SUCCEEDS, thirty lines above the defect; **(b) the failed-device arm never sets PFSR** while OpenBSD&#x27;s `trap.c` READS `CMMU_PFSR_FAULT(tf_dpfsr)` -- so a naked `return 0` is a HALF-FAULT, an exception without the bus-error status the guest inspects; **(c) the same CPU and the same class were already adjudicated the other way** -- `pcc2defaultkill` `#501` declined unimplemented offsets with a latched `(once)` and `return 1`, and `dev_m8820x.c` `#433` says it in product comments, complain-and-continue, because `#220` assigned HALT to &#x27;the guest asked the machine to halt&#x27; and CONTINUE to &#x27;model gap&#x27;; **(d) FAULT on a MAPPED hole is a kernel PANIC, not a clean probe** -- OpenBSD `T_DATAFLT` with no `pcb_onfault` goes to `panictrap`, and TAS / RESET_CPU0 / PC_BASE are all mapped.  A driver that stores without `badaddr()` dies. *** AND THE WARNING THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE FIX: deleting the halt and leaving `return 0` would SILENTLY CHANGE EVERY UNIMPLEMENTED ACCESS from &#x27;stop the emulator&#x27; to &#x27;inject DATA_ACCESS&#x27;.  **That is a behaviour change to the guest, not a de-fatalisation** -- and it is precisely the kind of change the addition licence refuses without a measured delta. *** **THE SHIPPING TABLE (proposed, not ratified):** `RESET_CPU_ALL` **KEEPS THE HALT** -- OpenBSD `boot()` writes it to reboot, so the guest asked, which is `#220`/footbridge_reset; the `default:` arm gets **member 3**, latched announce plus **`break` and never an early `return`** because the common tail writes `odata` on reads; TAS / `RESET_CPU0..3` / `SOFT_INT_FLAG` / `PIO0C` are **member 7 FILED, not in-round**, being real registers whose implementation needs a measured delta or a transferred warrant; and FAULT stays **available-but-not-chosen** unless someone measures OpenBSD&#x27;s `badaddr()` against both a `return 0` and a `PFSR_BERROR` poke. *** `RESET_CPU_ALL` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT MAKES THE SIBLING TEST WORK: it proves this file ALREADY distinguishes a deliberate halt from an accidental one, so the `default:` arm is not &#x27;how this file does things&#x27; -- it is the outlier. ***</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass inside it is about **148 KB** -- four range handlers (FUSE_ROM 1 KB, NVRAM 0x1FDC, NVRAM_ADDR_88K2 0x1FDC byte-only, TRI_PORT_RAM ~128 KB) plus **34 exact switch addresses**.  But the set that MATTERS is three nested sets, not one: **(1) COLD DEBUGGER, TRANSLATION OFF** -- `APR_V` 