hat list cannot be lost the way the first half was; its substance is NOT re-derived here and is owed.  *The point of this row is the pattern, not the defect: two items from one &#x27;file as rows&#x27; instruction, eleven days old, neither queued.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — Named alongside `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the same `lunaabsentcpu` &#x27;file as rows&#x27; list, and **never given a ledger id either** -- verified absent from both ledger.json and OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.  Filed as a PLACEHOLDER so the second half of that list cannot be lost the way the first half was; its substance is NOT re-derived here and is owed.  *The point of this row is the pattern, not the defect: two items from one &#x27;file as rows&#x27; instruction, eleven days old, neither queued.* — evidence: 2026-09-06 lunaabsentcpu pass-2/pass-3 panels; ledger + bugfile absence verified</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mapledmahang</span><span class="ttl">maple_do_dma_xfer() is for(;;) with its only exit gated on a bit read from guest RAM, so a guest that never sets it hangs the emulator inside a register write -- alive, running, and never coming back</span><span class="tip">maple_do_dma_xfer() is for(;;) with its only exit gated on a bit read from guest RAM, so a guest that never sets it hangs the emulator inside a register write -- alive, running, and never coming back — note: *** AN UNBOUNDED, GUEST-TRIGGERABLE LOOP INSIDE A DEVICE ACCESS HANDLER.  THE EMULATOR NEVER DIES -- IT NEVER COMES BACK. ***  `maple_do_dma_xfer()` is `for (;;)` at `dev_dreamcast_maple.c:409`, and its ONLY exit is `:535` `if (last_message) break;` where `last_message = buf[3] &amp; 0x80` (`:429`) is read **from guest RAM** at `d-&gt;dmaaddr`.  A guest that never sets bit 7 of that byte loops forever; `:532` only advances `addr`.  Triggered by an ordinary register write -- `case 0x18 MAPLE_STATE`, `case 1: maple_do_dma_xfer(cpu, d)` at `:588` -- after MAPLE_ENABLE and MAPLE_DMAADDR.  Machine: **dreamcast**. **THIS IS A THIRD CLASS AND THE TREE HAS NO NAME FOR IT.**  An `exit(`/`abort()` is a HOST DEATH; `cpu-&gt;running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT where the process lives and the strand stops. **This is neither: the process is alive, the strand is running, and the user&#x27;s machine is gone.**  No sweep in this project looks for it, because there is no keyword -- which is exactly the property that let the `abort()` class survive three censuses. **NOT A DUPLICATE, verified:** `mapledmaaddr` is about the DMAADDR read value and `mapledmaadvance` about address advancement past the command.  Neither is the loop bound. There IS an `exit(1)` at `:426` inside the loop, on a different condition -- so the function has a terminating path and still has no trip cap. DISPOSITION NOT DECIDED HERE.  The obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop), but the bound is a design question: a legitimate maple chain has a real maximum and picking it needs the Comstedt reverse-engineering note the function header cites, not a guess.  **The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never a number that feels safe.**  *** THE BOUND IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THIS TREE, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN A FAILURE TO FIND ONE. ***  Comstedt&#x27;s own DMA paragraph -- the page this file&#x27;s header names -- says the controller *&#x27;will send ANY NUMBER of requests&#x27;*.  The &#x27;configurable amount of time&#x27; in that sentence is **per request**, and it is ALREADY in the model as `d-&gt;timeout` (MAPLE_SPEED bits 16-31), **stored and never read inside `maple_do_dma_xfer`**.  On hardware a timeout writes 0xFFFFFFFF at the result address and CONTINUES to the next descriptor -- it does not end the list.  **`d-&gt;timeout` therefore cannot be turned into a frame cap without inventing a meaning the register does not have.** **24 IS THE ENDPOINT CENSUS, NOT THE LIST CEILING** (4 ports x 6 units): a legal DMA may send DEVINFO *and* GETCOND to the same unit, or a burst of VMU BWRITEs.  Linux&#x27;s `MAPLE_MAXPACKETS 8` and the 16 KiB `MAPLE_DMA_SIZE` that Linux and KallistiOS both allocate are **SOFTWARE** batching, not ASIC limits, and neither is in this tree&#x27;s model. *** AND IT CORRECTED THE DISPOSITION I WROTE INTO THIS ROW WHEN I FILED IT. ***  I wrote *&#x27;the obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop)&#x27;*.  **The seven-member option set is the WRONG MENU here: every member presumes an ACCESS with a value to refuse, and this is a LIST WALK whose only exit is a guest bit.**  Bounding the walk is a NEW member -- *&#x27;do not dress it up as member 3 applied to a loop and then pick 24&#x27;*. **THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT, GREPPED RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT DOES NOT TRANSFER:** `dev_sgi_mec.c`&#x27;s `for (;;)` IS bounded -- by `dma_ptr_nr &gt;= 4` (the hardware has four DMA pointers) AND `j &gt;= MAX_TX_PACKET_LEN`, and `#203` added the outer cap after an inner `break` left the outer loop writing past the array.  **But that bound comes from the HOST STORAGE the fill writes into, and maple writes GUEST RAM at `receive_addr` -- there is no host buffer to cap against.**  *And that answers the sighunt&#x27;s open question: there is NO other `for (;;)` and no `while (1)` in `src/devices/*.c`; the sighunt&#x27;s second one is mec, and mec is bounded.* **THE DISPOSITION, WITHOUT INVENTING N:** detect the **MISSING LAST**, not &#x27;too many devices&#x27; -- the defect is a list that never terminates, not a list longer than 24.  **DECLINE THE WHOLE DMA**, which is the shape this same function already uses for a malformed DMA: the unaligned `addr &amp; 0x1f` check prints and RETURNS with no `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT`.  *** TRUNCATING MID-LIST AND THEN FIRING DMADONE WOULD HAND THE GUEST A SILENT PARTIAL TRANSFER, and the unaligned path pointedly does not do that. ***  Latch the announce with `(once)` -- the unaligned path `fatal()`s EVERY time with no latch, which is floodclass.  **Do NOT `exit(1)`** (that converts a hang into a host death, the thing this queue is removing).  **Do NOT `cpu-&gt;running = 0`** (wrong class -- that is `lunafbhalt`/`8259cmdhalt`).  Do not make the DMA asynchronous in this round: that IS the hardware shape and would stop taking the host away, but it is a rewrite, and on real silicon a no-LAST list may still hang the Maple ASIC. **`:426` IS NOT A MODEL FOR MISSING LAST.**  All four `exit(1)`s in this function (`:426` GUN bit, `:459` multiple units, `:520` GETCOND function, `:529` unknown command) are UNIMPLEMENTED-PATH TRIPWIRES -- member 7&#x27;s tell, a stub with no predicate refusing a value. *** BWRITE IS THE TELL: it is `fatal` + `break` OF THE SWITCH, and then the loop CONTINUES. So a no-LAST list of BWRITE HANGS while a no-LAST list of an unimplemented command DIES -- the same loop, two different classes, decided by `cmd`. ***  The author used `exit(1)` for &#x27;I have not written this opcode&#x27;, not for &#x27;this list is malformed&#x27;. **Q4 -- IT CAN BE WITNESSED, AND AN EXISTING PROBE IS A LIVE HAZARD.**  Death-keyed oracles are all blind here: `exit(`, `abort()`, WIFSIGNALED and EOF-on-pty none of them fire.  *** AND `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py`&#x27;s `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` AFTER `rd(10.0)` WOULD BLOCK FOREVER ON THIS BUG -- that probe cannot be the detector, and if it ever reaches this path it hangs the harness rather than reporting anything. ***  The instrument is SIGKILL-after-deadline plus a LAST-set control; the enable and alignment checks both return before the loop, so they are the liveness controls. **WHAT WOULD SETTLE N:** measuring the real ASIC with lists of 24, 25 and 512 frames 