angs**, reporting nothing at all.  **This is the mirror of the false-green class closed today as `probedeathoracle`.**  That one was about a probe reporting ALIVE for a dead process; this is a probe that cannot report anything because it waits forever on a process that will not die.  Both come from keying an oracle on process death without a deadline -- and `probedeathoracle`&#x27;s census keyed on `WNOHANG`, so a BLOCKING `waitpid` was outside its population by construction. **The remedy is the same shape as the one already applied: a DEADLINE.**  A blocking `waitpid` in a harness that can drive a non-terminating guest path needs SIGKILL-after-N rather than patience.  Filed rather than fixed: it is a real hazard on a path nothing currently drives, and the fix belongs with whichever round builds the `mapledmahang` witness, because that round is the one that will first make the path reachable.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py` CALLS `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` -- A BLOCKING WAIT -- AFTER `rd(10.0)`, AND `mapledmahang` IS A PATH ON WHICH THE EMULATOR NEVER EXITS. ***  If that probe ever drives a maple DMA list with no LAST bit, the blocking wait **never returns and the harness hangs**, reporting nothing at all.  **This is the mirror of the false-green class closed today as `probedeathoracle`.**  That one was about a probe reporting ALIVE for a dead process; this is a probe that cannot report anything because it waits forever on a process that will not die.  Both come from keying an oracle on process death without a deadline -- and `probedeathoracle`&#x27;s census keyed on `WNOHANG`, so a BLOCKING `waitpid` was outside its population by construction. **The remedy is the same shape as the one already applied: a DEADLINE.**  A blocking `waitpid` in a harness that can drive a non-terminating guest path needs SIGKILL-after-N rather than patience.  Filed rather than fixed: it is a real hazard on a path nothing currently drives, and the fix belongs with whichever round builds the `mapledmahang` witness, because that round is the one that will first make the path reachable. — evidence: regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py; regress/artifacts/sighunt_20260917/MAPLEBOUND_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mpipirealloc</span><span class="ttl">dev_mp.c reallocs the IPI queue larger on every IPI write with no cap, and only the target CPU&#x27;s own read drains it -- host heap grows by accretion</span><span class="tip">dev_mp.c reallocs the IPI queue larger on every IPI write with no cap, and only the target CPU&#x27;s own read drains it -- host heap grows by accretion — note: *** AN UNCAPPED HOST ALLOCATION THAT A GUEST GROWS BY ACCRETION -- THE SUCCESS-CASE DENIAL, NOT A FAILED MALLOC. ***  `dev_mp.c:198-200`: a write to `DEV_MP_IPI_ONE` or `DEV_MP_IPI_MANY` does `d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i]++` and then `CHECK_ALLOCATION(d-&gt;ipi[i] = realloc(d-&gt;ipi[i], d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i] * sizeof(int)))` -- **with no cap of any kind**.  Verified at HEAD. **NOTHING DRAINS THE QUEUE EXCEPT THE TARGET CPU READING IT.**  The only consumer is `DEV_MP_IPI_READ` at `:211`, which decrements `n_pending_ipis[cpu-&gt;cpu_id]` -- i.e. the *receiving* CPU&#x27;s own read.  A guest that sends IPIs and never reads them grows the host heap four bytes at a time, **with a `realloc` on every single write**, so the cost is both resident memory and repeated copying. REACHABILITY: **the test machines ALWAYS add it** -- `machine_test.c:148` and `:333` both `device_add(&quot;mp addr=... irq=...&quot;)`.  So this is not a device nobody installs. **SEVERITY LOW, RANKED BELOW `guestloopdenial`, and the reason is the shape rather than the size:** this is not a 2 GB one-shot allocation.  It is growth by accretion -- many writes, four bytes each -- so it is slower to matter than a tight loop and needs sustained guest cooperation.  It sits in the same family as `guestloopdenial` (the guest takes the host away without killing it) but arrives by a different road: **resident memory grows instead of a loop spinning.** *** NOTE WHAT `CHECK_ALLOCATION` DOES AND DOES NOT BUY HERE.  It turns a FAILED realloc into a clean `exit(1)`, which is why this is not a crash -- but it does nothing at all about a long run of SUCCESSFUL ones, which is the whole defect.  A guard against failure is not a guard against growth. ***  **ADJACENT, AND FLAGGED HONESTLY AS OUTSIDE THE DIRECTORIES THE HUNT WAS ASKED ABOUT:** `scsi_transfer_allocbuf()` in `src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c` is the malloc that SCSI controllers actually use, and devices pass guest lengths into it.  READ/WRITE is capped at **64 MiB** by `#163`&#x27;s `SCSI_MAX_XFER_BYTES` with a 64-bit multiply from `#133`.  But `dev_mb89352.c`&#x27;s `transfer_count` (a `size_t` fed from the 24-bit TCH/TCM/TCL register triple) is used as `want_len` for DATA OUT **and for the CDB**, giving a command buffer of up to **16,777,215 bytes** on a FIELD-WIDTH cap with no software cap behind it.  Machine: luna88k.  Recorded here rather than filed as its own row because it is a bound, not an overflow, and it belongs with whoever next touches mb89352 -- which `b120` already owns.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class