HE AUDIT&#x27;S OWN NUMBERS WERE TRUNCATED GREPS THAT SAID SO -- WHICH IS WHY THEY WERE RE-DERIVED AND NOT TRANSCRIBED. ***  It reported `&gt;=1999` ev fields and `&gt;=999` _scratchpad citations, flagging both as cut off, and gave 297 as COMPLETE.  The walk reproduces **297 exactly** and shows the two lower bounds were low by ~1.6x and ~1.9x.  *An honest lower bound is a good record and a bad number: it is indistinguishable from a measurement once it is quoted one hop away from the tool that produced it*, which is this project&#x27;s frozen-number class arriving through the seat rather than through the author. **WHY NO CHECKER SEES IT, and it is a scope boundary rather than a checker defect.**  `check_cited_artifacts.py` exists and does exactly this job -- but its own header sets the altitude deliberately: *&quot;checks committed records, never scratchpad state&quot;*, per the fable5 `#462` Decision 4c.  It reads CHANGELOG ROUND BLOCKS, not ledger `ev` fields.  So the 297 are outside it BY DESIGN, and widening it is a scope change the adjudication that created it explicitly bounded. **RANKED LOW, and the reason is the same one the carrier&#x27;s own citation-form ruling gives: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT READS AN `ev` FIELD FUNCTIONALLY.**  It is consumed by a reader, and a reader following a `pipeline/evidence/...` path gets an immediate, loud failure -- not a wrong answer.  That is the SAFE direction.  The cost is audit expense and the fact that 27 rows cannot be verified from their own fields.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">