d><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ledgercommitsshape</span><span class="ttl">ledger `commits` is a space-separated string in 382 rows and a list in 37, so a consumer that assumes a list silently yields CHARACTERS and renders one SHA as seven commits</span><span class="tip">ledger `commits` is a space-separated string in 382 rows and a list in 37, so a consumer that assumes a list silently yields CHARACTERS and renders one SHA as seven commits — note: *** THE LEDGER&#x27;S `commits` FIELD IS A SPACE-SEPARATED STRING IN 382 ROWS AND A LIST IN 37, AND A CONSUMER THAT ASSUMES A LIST FAILS SILENTLY BY YIELDING CHARACTERS.  Measured 2026-09-17 -- by falling into it. ***  `&quot;, &quot;.join(row[&quot;commits&quot;])` on a string row renders `7a41b3f` as `7, a, 4, 1, b, 3, f`, which **looks like a list of seven commits** rather than like an error.  Census over 512 rows: `str` 382, `NoneType` 93, `list` 37; 132 of the strings are non-empty. **IT IS NOT A DATA DEFECT -- both shapes are parseable and the shipped tools handle them** (`gen_dashboard.py` and the checkers all work today).  It is a SCHEMA INCONSISTENCY that makes every new consumer a coin-flip, and the failure mode is the one this project keeps naming: **a plausible wrong answer that does not look like a failure.**  The records audit&#x27;s own Q1 had to count &quot;hash-bearing rows&quot; precisely because the field has two shapes. **DISPOSITION: FILED, NOT NORMALISED.**  Rewriting 382 rows to lists is a mechanical edit to the single source of truth with no measured defect behind it -- the ADDITION LICENCE refuses &quot;cannot hurt&quot;, and this is its records-side twin.  The cheap durable fix is a one-line accessor (`commits_of(row)` returning a list from either shape) adopted where consumers are written, which is FILED as a tooling change owing its own negative controls. *Recorded because the near-miss is the instructive part: the wrong output was nine rows of single letters in a brief-building extraction, and it was obvious ONLY because a SHA is recognisable.  A field of short tokens would have rendered the same way and read as real.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE LEDGER&#x27;S `commits` FIELD IS A SPACE-SEPARATED STRING IN 382 ROWS AND A LIST IN 37, AND A CONSUMER THAT ASSUMES A LIST FAILS SILENTLY BY YIELDING CHARACTERS.  Measured 2026-09-17 -- by falling into it. ***  `&quot;, &quot;.join(row[&quot;commits&quot;])` on a string row renders `7a41b3f` as `7, a, 4, 1, b, 3, f`, which **looks like a list of seven commits** rather than like an error.  Census over 512 rows: `str` 382, `NoneType` 93, `list` 37; 132 of the strings are non-empty. **IT IS NOT A DATA DEFECT -- both shapes are parseable and the shipped tools handle them** (`gen_dashboard.py` and the checkers all work today).  It is a SCHEMA INCONSISTENCY that makes every new consumer a coin-flip, and the failure mode is the one this project keeps naming: **a plausible wrong answer that does not look like a failure.**  The records audit&#x27;s own Q1 had to count &quot;hash-bearing rows&quot; precisely because the field has two shapes. **DISPOSITION: FILED, NOT NORMALISED.**  Rewriting 382 rows to lists is a mechanical edit to the single source of truth with no measured defect behind it -- the ADDITION LICENCE refuses &quot;cannot hurt&quot;, and this is its records-side twin.  The cheap durable fix is a one-line accessor (`commits_of(row)` returning a list from either shape) adopted where consumers are written, which is FILED as a tooling change owing its own negative controls. *Recorded because the near-miss is the instructive part: the wrong output was nine rows of single letters in a brief-building extraction, and it was obvious ONLY because a SHA is recognisable.  A field of short tokens would have rendered the same way and read as real.* — evidence: tools/pipeline/ledger.json -- type census: str 382, NoneType 93, list 37</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4dmacnote</span><span class="ttl">sh4_dmac_transfer signals transfer end through no channel -- it never sets CHCR_TE nor clears DE, while dev_pvr.c does; a guest polling CHCR on the generic DMAC path would never see completion, IF such a path transfers</span><span class="tip">sh4_dmac_transfer signals transfer end through no channel -- it never sets CHCR_TE nor clears DE, while dev_pvr.c does; a guest polling CHCR on the generic DMAC path would never see completion, IF such a path transfers — note: *** THE COMMENT IS WRONG; WHETHER THE BEHAVIOUR IS WRONG IS **NOT** MEASURED, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE ROW. ***  `sh4_dmac_transfer` never sets `CHCR_TE` and never clears `CHCR_DE`, while `dev_pvr.c` sets TE on its own DMA path.  A guest that polls CHCR for transfer end on the GENERIC DMAC path would therefore never see it. **WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT CLAIMED:** `#453`&#x27;s own analysis records that the only RS encoding reaching this point is the SINGLE-ADDRESS EXTERNAL case, **which performs no transfer** -- so &quot;never signals completion&quot; may be exactly right for the one path that arrives.  *Not signalling the end of a transfer that did not happen is not a defect.* **WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, and it is the ordinary ladder question:** a rung-3 probe that drives the generic DMAC on a path which DOES transfer, then reads CHCR back through `DEVICE_ACCESS` and asks whether TE is set.  If no such path is reachable, the honest answer is that the arm is unreachable and the row closes as documented-only -- which is a real answer, not a failure. *Ranked LOW: it is a HANG-class candidate (the third defect class this queue tracks) with no witness and a named reason to think it may be vacuous.  It ranks below every measured defect and is recorded so the question is not lost a second time.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — *** THE COMMENT IS WRONG; WHETHER THE BEHAVIOUR IS WRONG IS **NOT** MEASURED, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE ROW. ***  `sh4_dmac_transfer` never sets `CHCR_TE` and never clears `CHCR_DE`, while `dev_pvr.c` sets TE on its own DMA path.  A guest that polls CHCR for transfer end on the GENERIC DMAC path would therefore never see it. **WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT CLAIMED:** `#453`&#x27;s own analysis records that the only RS encoding reaching this point is the SINGLE-ADDRESS EXTERNAL case, **which performs no transfer** -- so &quot;never signals completion&quot; may be exactly right for the one path that arrives.  *Not signalling the end of a transfer that did not happen is not a defect.* **WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, and it is the ordinary ladder question:** a rung-3 probe that drives the generic DMAC on a path which DOES transfer, then reads CHCR back through `DEVICE_ACCESS` and asks whether TE is set.  If no such path is reachable, the honest answer is that the arm is unreachable and the row closes as documented-only -- which is a real answer, not a failure. *Ranked LOW: it is a HANG-class candidate (the third defect class this queue tracks) with no witness and a named reason to think it may be vacuous.  It ranks below every measured defect and is recorded so the question is not lost a second time.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/grok.txt; src/devices/dev_sh4.c; src/devices/dev_pvr.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmaflood</span><span class="ttl">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c prints an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() on the ACCEPTED-DMA path, so ordinary correct guest traffic floods the log and -q cannot silence it -- a floodclass site the floodclass row does not name</span><span class="tip">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c prints an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() on the ACCEPTED-DMA path, so ordinary correct guest traffic floods the log and -q cannot silence it -- a floodclass site the floodclass row does not name — note: *** AN UNLATCHED GUEST-DRIVABLE `fatal()` ON THE **SUCCESS** PATH -- IT FIRES ON EVERY ACCEPTED DMA, NOT ON A REFUSAL.  Verified 2026-09-17 by brace depth, not by eye. ***  `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` prints `[ dreamcast_gdrom_dma: Transfering %i bytes to 0x%08x (guest asked for %i) ]` on the accepted-transfer path with **no latch of any kind**. **HOW IT WAS SETTLED, because the obvious check is misleading here:** the enclosing function DOES contain a latch -- `if (!(d-&gt;warned &amp; GDROM_W_DMANODATA))` -- about thirty lines above the print, so a reader scanning for *&quot;is there a `d-&gt;warned` test nearby&quot;* finds one and stops.  **Brace depth settles it: that block CLOSES before the print**, which sits outside it (depth -1 relative to the latch&#x27;s `if`).  *A latch in the same function is not a latch on this line, and the two are indistinguishable without counting braces.* **WHY THE SUCCESS PATH IS THE BAD PLACE FOR IT.**  Every other flood this queue tracks is on a REFUSAL arm, where the guest has to do something unusual to provoke it.  This one is on the path a working driver takes, so **ordinary correct guest behaviour produces the flood** -- and `fatal()` has no `-q` early-out, so it cannot be silenced by verbosity. **NOT INTRODUCED BY `#462`, AND THE SEAT SAID SO ITSELF** (*&quot;This is not introduced by the diff -- the original also had it&quot;*).  `#462` MOVED the print below the `d-&gt;data == NULL` check and changed it to print `d-&gt;data_len` rather than the guest&#x27;s register, which is why the line is correct about its CONTENT and still unlatched about its FREQUENCY. *** `floodclass` DOES NOT COVER THIS SITE: checked, and the row mentions neither `gdrom`, `dreamcast` nor `Transfering`. ***  So it is a genuinely new member of a class this project already tracks, rather than a duplicate. **DISPOSITION IS NOT OBVIOUS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHOSEN HERE.**  The house idiom is member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- but this is not a decline, it is a SUCCESS, and a `(once)` latch on a success path silences the transfer log after the first DMA, which may be the diagnostic someone wants.  The option set for a NOISY-BUT-CORRECT print is not the de-fatalisation set, and naming the right menu is the first job of whoever takes this. *Provenance: the deepseek seat, `panel_20260917_100431`, in an answer STAMPED ACROSS SIX ROWS -- so the finding had no cell of its own.  Third real defect recovered today from a review that was written, delivered and never read as its own answer.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row