 contain a latch -- `if (!(d-&gt;warned &amp; GDROM_W_DMANODATA))` -- about thirty lines above the print, so a reader scanning for *&quot;is there a `d-&gt;warned` test nearby&quot;* finds one and stops.  **Brace depth settles it: that block CLOSES before the print**, which sits outside it (depth -1 relative to the latch&#x27;s `if`).  *A latch in the same function is not a latch on this line, and the two are indistinguishable without counting braces.* **WHY THE SUCCESS PATH IS THE BAD PLACE FOR IT.**  Every other flood this queue tracks is on a REFUSAL arm, where the guest has to do something unusual to provoke it.  This one is on the path a working driver takes, so **ordinary correct guest behaviour produces the flood** -- and `fatal()` has no `-q` early-out, so it cannot be silenced by verbosity. **NOT INTRODUCED BY `#462`, AND THE SEAT SAID SO ITSELF** (*&quot;This is not introduced by the diff -- the original also had it&quot;*).  `#462` MOVED the print below the `d-&gt;data == NULL` check and changed it to print `d-&gt;data_len` rather than the guest&#x27;s register, which is why the line is correct about its CONTENT and still unlatched about its FREQUENCY. *** `floodclass` DOES NOT COVER THIS SITE: checked, and the row mentions neither `gdrom`, `dreamcast` nor `Transfering`. ***  So it is a genuinely new member of a class this project already tracks, rather than a duplicate. **DISPOSITION IS NOT OBVIOUS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHOSEN HERE.**  The house idiom is member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- but this is not a decline, it is a SUCCESS, and a `(once)` latch on a success path silences the transfer log after the first DMA, which may be the diagnostic someone wants.  The option set for a NOISY-BUT-CORRECT print is not the de-fatalisation set, and naming the right menu is the first job of whoever takes this. *Provenance: the deepseek seat, `panel_20260917_100431`, in an answer STAMPED ACROSS SIX ROWS -- so the finding had no cell of its own.  Third real defect recovered today from a review that was written, delivered and never read as its own answer.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_deepseek.txt; src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c -- the `Transfering&#x27; fatal, measured OUTSIDE the GDROM_W_DMANODATA block by brace depth</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wallverdictand</span><span class="ttl">seat_verdicts.py suppresses a genuine wall when the seat streamed a verdict-shaped phrase -- `and not v` applies the WEAK-pattern condition to STRONG ones too, against the file&#x27;s own documented rule; 2 of 166 walls hidden</span><span class="tip">seat_verdicts.py suppresses a genuine wall when the seat streamed a verdict-shaped phrase -- `and not v` applies the WEAK-pattern condition to STRONG ones too, against the file&#x27;s own documented rule; 2 of 166 walls hidden — note: *** `seat_verdicts.py` CAN SCORE A GENUINE WALL AS `answered` WHEN THE SEAT STREAMED ENOUGH TEXT TO YIELD A VERDICT-SHAPED PHRASE.  Measured 2026-09-17. ***  `is_walled = _is_wall(t[-600:], t[-4000:]) and not v`.  The tail test is right and fires; `and not v` suppresses it. **IT CONTRADICTS THE FILE&#x27;S OWN DOCUMENTED RULE**, which distinguishes *&quot;STRONG ones are a notice on their own; WEAK ones (`quota`, `rate limit`) need&quot;* the extra condition -- and then applies the extra condition to both.  `usage limit` and `quota will reset` are both in `WALL_STRONG`. **MEASURED POPULATION, not generalised from the instance:** 2,369 seat files / 356 panel dirs; **166 walls by the tail test; 2 hidden** -- `panel_20260917_085545/kimi.txt` (2.4 MB, today, on the in-flight `#530`) and `panel_20260820_005715/codex.txt` (21 KB). **WHY THE GUARD EXISTS, so nobody removes it blindly:** the file records that the wall test twice fired on LIVE seats that were merely DISCUSSING rate limits.  The fix for that was the TAIL RESTRICTION (`t[-600:]`), which is the real discriminator; `and not v` is belt-and-braces that now costs more than it buys for STRONG patterns. **THE SHAPE OF A FIX, filed rather than applied:** report WALLED on a STRONG pattern in the tail regardless of `v`, and keep `and not v` for WEAK patterns only -- which is exactly what the file already says it does.  It owes two negative controls (a live seat discussing quotas must NOT be walled; a strong-notice tail with a verdict in the stream MUST be) and it is a third checker change in one session, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><sp