="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seatprescriptiondiff</span><span class="ttl">a row prescribed BY NAME in a seat&#x27;s fix-design answer can be lost even when #490&#x27;s diff-against-the-PRETABLE rule is obeyed, because the prescription never entered the PRETABLE -- and a PRETABLE that mis-classifies the cell CERTIFIES the omission</span><span class="tip">a row prescribed BY NAME in a seat&#x27;s fix-design answer can be lost even when #490&#x27;s diff-against-the-PRETABLE rule is obeyed, because the prescription never entered the PRETABLE -- and a PRETABLE that mis-classifies the cell CERTIFIES the omission — note: *** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: &#x27;5. `isr 20 rd&#x27; / `isr 28 rd&#x27; -- load does not clobber ISR; load&#x27;s VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don&#x27;t-mirror)&#x27;.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd val&#x27; and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the same pattern: grok&#x27;s design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER&#x27;S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is &#x27;a row list rewritten from a panel&#x27;s findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block&#x27;s own prescribed rows, not merely extended with the new ones.&#x27;  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in a SEAT&#x27;S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row 