f ip30abortkill.  Two structurally identical `fatal(&quot;TODO: ip22 legacy interrupt rewrite!&quot;); abort();&#x27; pairs in dev_sgi_ip22.c, in the WRITE arms of the local0 and local1 interrupt masks.  *** THE CENSUS LESSON RECURS: these carry no `exit(&#x27;, so the same three exit( sweeps that walked past #531&#x27;s four sites walk past these too.  A census keyed on `exit(&#x27; cannot see a host death spelled abort(). ***  Ranked HIGH on the same warrant as #531: guest-reachable host death on an advertised machine. The disposition is likely the same as #531&#x27;s -- heart.h has no ip22 equivalent, so the option-set work starts from the file&#x27;s own sibling handlers.  LINE NUMBERS VERIFIED 2026-09-17 by opening the file: :314 and :338 exactly, 2 abort() and 0 exit( in dev_sgi_ip22.c.  Grade upgraded BELIEVED -&gt; CONFIRMED.  *** SEVERITY CORRECTED HIGH -&gt; LOW THE SAME DAY IT WAS FILED, AND THE CORRECTION IS MINE TO OWN: I RANKED IT AS A SIBLING OF #531 WITHOUT CHECKING THE ONE FACT THAT MADE #531 LIVE. ***  #531&#x27;s warrant is that `-E sgi -e ip30&#x27; is an ADVERTISED machine AND the device is actually installed -- its rung-3 probe reached the code and killed the host, empirically.  dev_sgi_ip22 is DIFFERENT and the difference is decisive:  (a) `dev_sgi_ip22_init&#x27; has exactly TWO call sites in the whole tree, machine_sgi.c:270 and :278, and BOTH ARE COMMENTED OUT;  (b) the file defines DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip22) but NO DEVINIT(sgi_ip22), so the autodev / device_add route cannot register it either;  (c) it still COMPILES IN (Makefile.skel lists dev_sgi_ip22.o), which is why an abort()-census finds it and a reachability question does not get asked.  *** THE DEVICE IS NEVER IN ANY MACHINE&#x27;S MEMORY MAP, SO NO GUEST CAN REACH EITHER abort(). ***  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE WITNESS LADDER, and it is why this cannot simply be &#x27;done later&#x27;: NO WITNESS EXISTS OR CAN EXIST AT RUNGS 2-4 while the device is uninstalled, and installing it to reach the